WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.980
Welcome to Ear Biscuits,

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.590
the podcast where two lifelong friends

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.500
talk about life for a long time.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:11.333
I'm Link.

00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:12.166
And I'm Rhett.

00:00:12.166 --> 00:00:14.340
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.970
yeah, we're back at the
round table of dim lighting.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:18.803
Yep.

00:00:18.803 --> 00:00:19.636
We're gonna be talking about

00:00:19.636 --> 00:00:22.533
what really happened at MythiCon.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:24.353
Yeah, this is-

00:00:24.353 --> 00:00:26.220
As if there's a secret that
we've been holding onto.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.053
There's not really.

00:00:27.053 --> 00:00:27.979
Well, I think there's-

00:00:27.979 --> 00:00:29.172
It's just a sensational introduction.

00:00:29.172 --> 00:00:31.320
I mean, there's definitely
some behind the scenes.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.153
Oh yeah.

00:00:32.153 --> 00:00:33.990
Like, I mean, there's things
that we haven't talked about,

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.540
about our experience that, you know,

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.470
I think it's good to just put it down,

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.800
put down our experience,

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.330
to tape or whatever type
of digital ones and zeros

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:51.240
we record things to, for posterity.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.100
Yeah, because it's very fresh right now.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:53.933
So fresh.

00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:57.480
We just got back home,
just a couple of days ago.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.430
The wounds are still open.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.880
So the experience is very,
very fresh on our minds

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.590
and in our hearts.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:09.540
I would say that the
general feeling right now,

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.700
both the general feeling
while we were at MythiCon,

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.530
and then as MythiCon was coming to a close

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:18.530
and in these days after,
is that it was a success.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.240
Like, we're feeling really good

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:26.240
about the way that the Mythical
Beasts responded to it,

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.950
the work that the
Mythical crew put into it.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.720
I mean, the thing that we said all along

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.100
is that if we can create
a positive experience

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.770
for the majority of people who were there,

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:38.820
then we have succeeded.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:39.990
Because when you go out

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.270
and try to do something
for the first time,

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:43.103
that hasn't been done before...

00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:45.150
A convention? Especially.
... at least by us.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:45.983
Yeah.

00:01:45.983 --> 00:01:49.560
And, you know, something
like this specifically

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.830
hasn't been done in creatordom.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:57.060
This sort of more boutique event

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.470
or whatever you want to call it,

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.330
at a place that wasn't
a convention center.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:05.010
Boutique immersive two day experience.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.720
We're feeling good.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:07.560
I'm feeling good.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.830
That doesn't mean that there
weren't things that went wrong

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.980
and there weren't things that
could have been done better

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.377
and there's not constructive
criticism floating around,

00:02:17.377 --> 00:02:19.890
which we can talk a little bit about.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:23.230
But yeah, I mean, my first thing was

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.440
nobody died, was maimed, or
taken away on a stretcher.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:29.430
Honestly, yeah.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.890
So, I was just so glad because,

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:35.100
like, these conventions
have a reputation for, like,

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.190
really going south, you know.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.140
There's documentaries made about them.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.890
Well, as the days approached, I mean,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.320
that is one of the things
I wanted to talk about,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.120
was just the way that we
were feeling about things

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.130
as the date was approaching.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:54.780
Right, and I'll say,

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.975
I also wanna talk about,
like, we will talk about

00:02:57.975 --> 00:03:00.870
what does this this mean
for the future of MythiCon?

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:01.890
What are our plans?

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.060
What do we know?

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:06.120
We know some things already,
so we can talk about that.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:06.953
Yep.

00:03:06.953 --> 00:03:09.780
So yeah, so you can go
back to your feelings now.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:14.490
I will say, ultimately, before
we start stepping through it,

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:17.100
I kind of feel like I'm
basking in the afterglow

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:21.360
of a milestone career moment.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.350
Like, just being able
to experience MythiCon

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:29.850
was extremely positive for me personally.

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:32.730
And I'm just so grateful that

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:36.300
we have a relationship
with the Mythical Beasts,

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.490
such that, like, we can all get excited

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.440
about creating something like this.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.980
And it was really special.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:47.476
Like, there were a number
of points where I was like,

00:03:47.476 --> 00:03:49.440
I think this is the peak for me.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.480
This is my favorite thing about MythiCon.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.370
And that happened probably five times.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:57.720
Well, the way I described it on Twitter,

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.300
I guess on Sunday, you know, or Monday,

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:01.500
right after it was over,

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.400
it represented a culmination
of this relationship

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:13.290
that we've been building for many years.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:18.290
I think that that is,
that's why I'm taking away,

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.120
despite some of the things
that were like lessons learned,

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.290
that we will take into the future,

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:23.940
which we were fully expecting, it's like,

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.110
you do something for the first time,

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:27.570
you're like, oh, this, this and that,

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.370
these are things that we would change,

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:30.780
these are things that
we would do differently.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:34.650
You go into a new venture
expecting to learn those lessons.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:36.900
But the thing that I was really hoping

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:39.120
would be the core experience of the event

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.930
would be that this relationship

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:45.090
that we've been building
with the Mythical Beasts,

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:49.860
and for some, that's a
over 10 year relationship.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.251
For some, that's a 10 week relationship

00:04:52.251 --> 00:04:54.510
and everything in between.

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:56.370
That it would feel-

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:57.600
Some are like 16 years.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.920
That the relationship
that had been building

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:06.810
and had been cultivated
was being expressed,

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:09.330
almost in the way that if you think about

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:11.790
you've got somebody, you
have a long distance,

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:13.860
and I don't wanna make this,
it's not like a romantic thing,

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:15.630
but a friendship, let's just say.

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:16.680
You meet somebody.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:17.513
A pen pal.

00:05:17.513 --> 00:05:19.770
And actually this is the
case with a lot of people

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:22.470
that we ended up talking
to in the meet and greet

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:23.400
and then in the brunch,

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:27.030
we got to interact a total of eight hours

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:31.110
over the course of two
days, two and a half days.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.030
We ended up having,

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:34.410
I don't know if you
thought about it like this,

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:38.610
eight hours of direct
interaction with Mythical Beasts.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.324
Like one on one, one on two, one on three.

00:05:41.324 --> 00:05:42.157
Oh wow.

00:05:43.223 --> 00:05:46.257
When you count your
James and the Shame set?

00:05:46.257 --> 00:05:48.014
No, no, I'm counting-

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:53.014
No, if you count James and
the Shame and your DJ set.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:54.570
For you and then my DJ set.

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:56.880
And then I'm talking, that's 10 hours.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:57.713
I'm saying-
Yeah.

00:05:57.713 --> 00:05:59.340
A two hour meet and greet on Friday,

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:01.740
a two hour meet and greet on Saturday

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.170
and then a four hour brunch on Sunday.

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:05.003
I don't know if you realized,

00:06:05.003 --> 00:06:07.320
we were there from 10
o'clock to two o'clock,

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.060
interacting with people individually,

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.160
going around going around to each table.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:12.480
Like it was a wedding reception.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:13.313
Yeah.

00:06:13.313 --> 00:06:16.800
And in one sense, it
was emotionally draining

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.580
because to have, sometimes,
intense interactions

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:22.500
with people who are, you know,

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.120
they've got a lot of things
that they've been feeling

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:25.200
and thinking for years

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:27.390
that now they have an
opportunity to tell you.

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:29.730
But for the most part,
including those things,

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:33.030
it was ultimately very energizing.

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:33.863
I agree, yeah.

00:06:33.863 --> 00:06:35.790
It's like you've got this pen pal

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:38.430
that you've been talking to for years.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:39.990
Who never writes you back,

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:41.460
but you know they're reading all of them.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:42.293
Yeah.

00:06:42.293 --> 00:06:43.126
And they might post things publicly.

00:06:43.126 --> 00:06:48.126
And then the relationship
is kinda consummated.

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:49.023
Don't do it.

00:06:49.023 --> 00:06:49.980
In person.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:51.150
And that's the funny thing

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:53.190
is we kept seeing people who
are like, yeah I've been,

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:55.590
this is a Mythical Beast
that I have a friendship with

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:57.510
and now we've been
hanging out all weekend.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:06:59.580
There's that that was
happening, which was beautiful

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:01.800
and which was why we
wanted to do it in person

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.300
and bring people together.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.730
But then there were the
interactions that we could have,

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:07.560
and not just with us,

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.420
everybody got to interact with the crew

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:11.580
both in a meet and greet setting,

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:14.160
but also just because
it was this little town.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:18.900
And I think that that was
the peak of it for me,

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:22.650
was seeing that this relationship that has

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:24.090
been building for so long

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:25.650
can kind of be expressed in a way

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:27.120
that you can only do in person.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.400
And, yeah, when you describe it as, like,

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.500
a boutique immersive experience,

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:36.500
that was what was so
exciting to see come together

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:39.900
because that was part
of our original idea.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:42.390
You know, it could have
been two years ago, right?

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:45.660
When we first hatched
the idea of MythiCon,

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:50.610
in the throes of the
pandemic and lockdown,

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:54.000
in our opening speech
from the rooftop where,

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.770
man, I could have fallen off that roof.

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:56.603
Yeah.

00:07:56.603 --> 00:07:58.110
Well the one instruction
that was given to Link,

00:07:58.110 --> 00:07:59.700
and there's gonna be a number of things

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:00.900
like this that come up, I'm sure.

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:03.168
But the one instruction
that was given to Link,

00:08:03.168 --> 00:08:07.740
at least five times, was when
you step out on this roof,

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:10.080
there will be a rug that's big enough

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:11.479
for the two of you to stand on.

00:08:11.479 --> 00:08:14.040
Don't step off of that,
because at that moment

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:15.900
you'll be on just like a tin roof

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:17.790
that may have condensation on it,

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:19.890
it's not, you know, it could be slippery.

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:22.020
And literally the first thing you did

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:23.370
was step off of the rug.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:26.340
Well I took a step and
then I took a second step

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:29.280
and then I was looking at
all the people down there

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:30.750
and then I looked down at my foot

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:33.510
and I remembered I'm
supposed to stay on the rug.

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:36.600
And when I looked down at my second step,

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:37.680
that foot that was out there,

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:39.510
I was like, there's no rug at all.

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:40.343
Where's the rug?

00:08:40.343 --> 00:08:42.240
And I realized, I had
already stepped over the rug.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:43.620
Yeah, it was the first thing.

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:44.453
It was just-

00:08:44.453 --> 00:08:45.510
There's only one step of the rug.

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:48.540
I pictured a rug going from the window

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:50.703
that we stepped out of onto that rooftop,

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:55.110
like, it's like a red carpet
going right to the edge.

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:56.520
Well, that's what you would picture.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:57.746
And then when I looked down,

00:08:57.746 --> 00:09:00.420
I was looking all around for the rug.

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:02.220
Then I looked behind me and saw the rug

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:03.360
and I saw you standing on it.

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:04.560
If you had...

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:05.393
I took a step back.

00:09:05.393 --> 00:09:06.990
If you had slipped off and, like,

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:07.860
I had to, like, go out there

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:08.693
and, like, hold you up,

00:09:08.693 --> 00:09:09.526
that would've been,
Would've been so awesome.

00:09:09.526 --> 00:09:10.440
first of all, everyone would've thought,

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:11.790
no, they wouldn't have
thought that it was planned.

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:12.660
They would've known.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:14.246
Because the other thing that was planned.

00:09:14.246 --> 00:09:15.079
We should have done that.

00:09:15.079 --> 00:09:16.230
The other thing that was planned,

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:17.970
a little behind the scenes here,

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:20.460
is that we were also told multiple times,

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:23.940
but unfortunately we weren't
told right before we did it,

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:27.150
that someone was planning a proposal

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:29.850
and that really, our only job was to...

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:30.900
Recognize this guy.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.700
Welcome everybody to MythiCon,

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:34.302
cover some logistics that actually

00:09:34.302 --> 00:09:35.640
we didn't even need to cover

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:37.170
because they were covered elsewhere.

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:40.710
And then just throw it over to this guy,

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:45.270
Wesley was his name,
Weatherholtz, Wesley Weatherholtz.

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:47.640
Still is his name.
Great name.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:48.930
Throw it over to Wesley
Unless he's taken her name.

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:50.880
and then Wesley would take it from there.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.280
He was like in a light, you know,

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:54.780
like, there was a basically, like,

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:56.462
he was under a light so
everybody could see him.

00:09:56.462 --> 00:09:57.431
And he was going to propose
He was waiting.

00:09:57.431 --> 00:09:58.318
to his girlfriend.
He was waiting

00:09:58.318 --> 00:10:00.060
our entire speech.

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:01.530
And our other job, I will say,

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:03.316
was to give a kick-ass welcome speech

00:10:03.316 --> 00:10:05.850
after Cotton Candy Randy introduced us.

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:08.550
And I think we delivered man.

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:09.383
Yeah, agreed.

00:10:09.383 --> 00:10:11.040
We had one microphone, we had to share it.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:12.900
We didn't get in a fight.

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:13.733
Almost did.

00:10:13.733 --> 00:10:16.110
I didn't go too off the rails.

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:17.130
Yeah.
I didn't weep.

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:18.345
You wore a pink hat.

00:10:18.345 --> 00:10:21.000
I found the pink hat,
up there in the attic.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.680
But yeah, he was waiting for our

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:24.420
kick-ass speeches to be done

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:28.920
and you know, just imagine
you're gonna propose,

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:30.930
like, I was so nervous
when I was gonna propose

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:33.420
and I did it two weeks early by accident.

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.607
You know, this guy was
like, I'm about to do it.

00:10:35.607 --> 00:10:37.680
All they gotta do is say my name

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:39.360
and then we go through the whole speech

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.010
and then we're like wrapping things up

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:42.957
and then we like say, "Tata for now",

00:10:42.957 --> 00:10:46.333
and we go back in and
he's just standing there.

00:10:46.333 --> 00:10:47.166
I felt so bad.

00:10:47.166 --> 00:10:48.510
I feel so bad for him.

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:50.400
Like, what we put him through by accident.

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:51.233
Yeah.
We just forgot.

00:10:51.233 --> 00:10:54.810
And the moment we duck our
heads back into the door,

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:59.700
Jenna's like, "The
proposal! Get back out!".

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:02.010
So we, like, turn around
before everybody disperses.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:02.850
It was like that quick.

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:03.683
No one started moving yet.

00:11:03.683 --> 00:11:05.490
But then you started
talking into the microphone,

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:06.417
which had been cut off.

00:11:06.417 --> 00:11:08.130
And the microphone was off.

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:08.963
The music was playing.

00:11:08.963 --> 00:11:09.796
People are dispersing.

00:11:09.796 --> 00:11:10.629
Now we're yelling,

00:11:10.629 --> 00:11:11.462
"Don't leave! Don't leave!"

00:11:11.462 --> 00:11:12.393
"Please don't leave!"

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:15.930
And that's like, so we ended
up handing it over to him.

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:17.790
I did say at that moment,

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:21.810
do not let this be indicative
of how MythiCon's gonna go.

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:23.220
Because, thankfully, we're actually

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:24.600
not in charge of the details.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:25.433
Like, this is one of the things

00:11:25.433 --> 00:11:26.850
that we were supposed to remember,

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:28.290
but our team is better at

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:29.850
remembering those kinds of details.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:31.110
I don't want to dog anybody,

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:33.780
but it kinda was indicative of MythiCon,

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:35.220
we'll get into that in a few ways,

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:38.220
that like some things
don't go exactly as planned

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:40.920
and you have to roll with
it and make the best of it.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:44.850
But back to what I was saying
when I was giving the speech,

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:47.820
what I told them there, that
I was saying again here,

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:50.770
is that like we hatched MythiCon

00:11:51.754 --> 00:11:53.610
in the throes of the pandemic.

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:56.370
We missed that interaction with fans.

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:59.310
You know, we had two years
of on and off touring

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:02.366
and we were like, let's do an event.

00:12:02.366 --> 00:12:05.820
Let's make something that is an experience

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:07.650
and we need to make it something

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:10.050
that is not just called a convention.

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:12.675
And it's not just where people show up

00:12:12.675 --> 00:12:16.710
to see us do a show alone

00:12:16.710 --> 00:12:20.700
or just watch people record their podcast.

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:22.350
Like, I just was kind of,

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:24.690
we were immediately
averse to just creating

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:27.420
what we had already
experienced at VidCon honestly,

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:29.550
but only for Mythical Beasts.

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:34.550
We wanted to do something
that was a holistic experience

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:37.790
and we thought the venue would
have a lot to do with that.

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:42.225
And it turns out we were
taking a lot of risks

00:12:42.225 --> 00:12:46.740
when we picked a venue that was outdoors

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:47.580
and a lot of those things.

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:50.910
But, like, we did it
because it was so charming

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:53.100
and unlike any other place you could go.

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:55.830
And once you showed up there, I mean,

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:57.540
it was kind of like being at

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:01.350
a very preliminary version of Disney,

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:03.060
if it was just us, you know,

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:06.870
like, on like a small primordial scale.

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:08.790
But that was kind of the idea.

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:10.563
So when fans, actually a
couple of fans were like,

00:13:10.563 --> 00:13:13.590
this is kind of like a Disney experience.

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:16.860
Because the team tried to think of

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:19.710
all the details along the way

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:23.160
and you know, if you read
the fine print on things,

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:24.720
you would see that there
were jokes on them.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:27.720
You know, that's kind of
an example of, you know,

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:31.020
yes this sign says 'do not
enter this particular building',

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:34.230
but then it also makes a joke about that.

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:35.063
I can't remember the jokes.

00:13:35.063 --> 00:13:36.180
A lot of them had to do with

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:37.294
you not being allowed in the building.

00:13:37.294 --> 00:13:38.127
Right.

00:13:38.127 --> 00:13:39.996
Which I found very funny.

00:13:39.996 --> 00:13:41.520
Yes, yes to that.

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:43.923
And I think that, incidentally,

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:47.949
most of the challenges and lessons learned

00:13:47.949 --> 00:13:49.650
are related to the fact

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:54.650
that we chose such a specific
venue that was isolated,

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:59.550
that required shuttling people to it.

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:02.659
That all your food options and everything,

00:14:02.659 --> 00:14:03.960
you're there, right?

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.090
You're kind of isolated on this island.

00:14:06.090 --> 00:14:08.910
We made a decision,
because we were going for

00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:11.670
this more memorable, unique experience

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:13.617
that created challenges that obviously,

00:14:13.617 --> 00:14:15.030
and we knew this going in,

00:14:15.030 --> 00:14:17.400
but challenges that you wouldn't have

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:20.460
if you were at a convention
center where you've got-

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:21.390
Hotel rooms.
A convention center

00:14:21.390 --> 00:14:24.240
is designed to have a convention at it.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:26.370
And transportation to a convention center,

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:28.410
a lot of times is just
walking from your hotel room,

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:29.250
which is somewhere.

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:32.850
If you want other
options for things to do,

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:34.110
you can just step right out

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:36.930
and go to things in a metro
area, that kind of thing.

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:38.580
So we understand that those are things

00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:40.626
that created challenges,

00:14:40.626 --> 00:14:42.720
but a convention center
is a convention center.

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:46.560
I'm not saying that if we,
you know, do this again,

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:48.030
it won't be at a place

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:49.590
that can accommodate
those kinds of things.

00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:51.180
Which I guess, this is
a good time to go ahead

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:53.249
and talk about the fact that

00:14:53.249 --> 00:14:57.270
we were planning on this
even before MythiCon,

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:58.323
just so you know.

00:14:59.289 --> 00:15:00.870
We had made a decision in the months

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:03.540
leading up to MythiCon 2022,

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:07.410
that we were not going
to do a MythiCon 2023,

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:10.440
because we wanted more than 12 months

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:14.130
in order to learn the
lessons that we learned in 22

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:16.500
and be able to apply them in 23.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:19.950
Because there's things
like selecting a location,

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:24.540
taking into account the amount
of the work that our team did

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:27.240
and how do you divide that
between the Mythical team.

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:28.860
And then we worked with, on location,

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:31.920
a great company that does festivals.

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:34.380
So, in order to give ourselves more runway

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:37.380
for lessons learned and
planning and moving forward,

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:38.370
we're just gonna go ahead and tell you

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:40.410
that there's not a MythiCon 23.

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:41.910
We already knew that going in,

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:43.939
but it's worth mentioning now

00:15:43.939 --> 00:15:45.510
as we're talking about this.

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:50.510
Again, because if we do it
again or when we do this again,

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:54.888
we want it to be done even better

00:15:54.888 --> 00:15:57.390
and be a step up from where we started.

00:15:57.390 --> 00:15:58.223
Yeah.

00:15:58.223 --> 00:16:00.539
So at this point, kind of,
everything's on the table

00:16:00.539 --> 00:16:04.770
in terms of, like, how we
follow up MythiCon 2022

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:08.970
with something that, the earliest
it would be would be 2024.

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:09.840
Right.

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:11.190
In terms of how we do it.

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:16.410
But I would, I just really,

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:19.080
I just consider the whole thing a success.

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:20.680
You know, I know that, you know,

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:23.250
it wasn't about the money, you know,

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:24.870
I'll also be honest about that.

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:29.310
You know, we, you know,
we didn't make money.

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:34.200
I'm just, you know, I think we broke even.

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:35.310
I don't think we broke even.

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:36.300
Oh, okay.

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:38.550
Well, I'm gonna think that
because it makes me feel...

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:40.020
We're still working through,

00:16:40.020 --> 00:16:42.780
we're still working through the finances,

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:45.690
but as of right now,
all indications are that

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:48.533
we paid to make MythiCon happen.

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:52.710
Which again...

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:53.940
I'm over here fixing my hair

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:55.290
because I don't want to hear.

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:57.420
Well, no, but again...

00:16:57.420 --> 00:16:58.253
It was worth it.

00:16:58.253 --> 00:16:59.190
It was totally worth it.

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:01.680
But also it wasn't a surprise.

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:05.230
Every single festival
that I've ever heard of

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:09.000
cost the people who
threw the festival money

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:10.470
the first time they did it.

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:11.303
We went in saying,

00:17:11.303 --> 00:17:14.100
"Listen, this is not gonna
be a money making venture.

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:16.980
Maybe one day it will
be, but this is not".

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:18.727
And people are probably thinking,

00:17:18.727 --> 00:17:22.260
"Man, for the money I paid for my ticket".

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:23.093
Yeah.

00:17:23.093 --> 00:17:23.926
"You still didn't make any money".

00:17:23.926 --> 00:17:25.350
But, like, yeah, like,
that's all part of it.

00:17:25.350 --> 00:17:26.850
There were lots of comments going around

00:17:26.850 --> 00:17:30.120
about another money grab
by Mythical, which again,

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:31.537
it's not the kind of thing
we're gonna come out and say,

00:17:31.537 --> 00:17:35.310
"Guys listen, we are paying to do this".

00:17:35.310 --> 00:17:36.360
But that is the fact.

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:37.193
Yeah.

00:17:37.193 --> 00:17:38.370
Is that it was not a money grab.

00:17:38.370 --> 00:17:40.410
There's lots of ways that
we could do a money grab,

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:43.320
if we chose to.

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:45.570
But it was starting,

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:46.770
it was beginning something,

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:48.270
just like in the book of Mythicality

00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:50.670
when we talk about pick
a direction and go,

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:52.620
just start doing something.

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:54.510
We have a tendency, and listen,

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:56.010
we do a lot of things that don't work

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:57.600
because we do a lot of things.

00:17:57.600 --> 00:17:58.530
And if you do a lot of things,

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:00.060
you're gonna do a lot of
things that don't work.

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:02.670
And we knew going into this that

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:06.030
it's very unlikely unless
we score like, again,

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:08.040
because, like, the, you know,

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:09.750
we called Liquid Death a sponsor.

00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:13.110
They were a sponsor in that
they gave us the water.

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:13.980
Which was huge.

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:15.280
We didn't get any money.

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:18.313
We would have to get a number of sponsors

00:18:18.313 --> 00:18:21.270
the festival world where we learned,

00:18:21.270 --> 00:18:23.250
is an interesting thing

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:24.420
and so now that we've done it

00:18:24.420 --> 00:18:25.740
and it was a positive experience

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:27.570
for the majority of people who were there,

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:30.060
next time maybe you can begin to introduce

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:32.520
those kinds of things that
help support it financially

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:35.070
in a way that it becomes
a money making venture.

00:18:35.070 --> 00:18:37.320
Which again, we would want it to be,

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:39.474
because we don't want to
pay out of our own pockets

00:18:39.474 --> 00:18:41.970
and lose money every time
we do something like this,

00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:43.620
regardless of how special it was.

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:46.290
But totally, totally fine for that

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:47.340
to be the case in the first one.

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:48.210
Yes.

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:51.870
And just take a quick aside
here, speaking of things

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:54.450
that we're not charging
Mythical Beasts for,

00:18:54.450 --> 00:18:58.170
but that are amazing and
edifying experiences,

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:00.903
I want to invite you to
go over to sporked.com.

00:19:00.903 --> 00:19:02.635
It's the website that we created

00:19:02.635 --> 00:19:06.120
where all of our taste tests

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:08.850
of anything you can buy
in the grocery store

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:12.390
are reflected in ranking
form and in articles.

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:15.690
And if there's, like, new types of snacks

00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:18.213
or grocery store items that you can try,

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:22.110
our team has tried it at Sporked

00:19:22.110 --> 00:19:23.190
and they're writing about it,

00:19:23.190 --> 00:19:24.330
so you can be exposed to

00:19:24.330 --> 00:19:25.257
what's the best thing to buy

00:19:25.257 --> 00:19:27.390
and what's the new fun things to buy.

00:19:27.390 --> 00:19:29.100
Go to sporked.com.

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:31.683
You don't have to pay a dime.

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:35.010
Oh, you just came up with a little jingle.

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:35.910
It's free.

00:19:35.910 --> 00:19:37.710
I never got back to talking about

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:40.082
how I was feeling in the days approaching.

00:19:40.082 --> 00:19:41.010
Yeah, get back to that.

00:19:41.010 --> 00:19:46.010
But I gotta say, with
every year that passes,

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:48.600
I feel like both of us get better

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:53.600
at just dealing with
increasing amounts of pressure

00:19:55.860 --> 00:19:58.590
to perform or for
something to be pulled off.

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:00.240
And eventually you just get to this point

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:01.860
where you're like, there's, I mean,

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:03.060
I'm doing everything I can

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:06.060
to make this everything that it can be

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:08.100
and everything that we want it to be.

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:09.270
But there's a number of things

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:11.040
that are outside of our control.

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:13.320
And just, you know, the
larger that Mythical gets

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:16.410
and the more things that we try,

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:18.510
the more kind of impervious you get

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:20.970
to just stepping into another experience

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:22.740
that you feel completely unqualified for.

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:26.010
I feel like every single year,

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:29.250
I'm just doing something else
that I feel unqualified for.

00:20:29.250 --> 00:20:31.076
My life has almost become

00:20:31.076 --> 00:20:33.690
doing things that I feel unqualified for.

00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:35.099
And so I've actually gotten better.

00:20:35.099 --> 00:20:35.932
Oh yeah.

00:20:35.932 --> 00:20:38.010
At not being nervous about things.

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:38.843
Definitely.

00:20:38.843 --> 00:20:42.990
But there was a lot of,
I think, hidden stress

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:47.160
that was popping up in certain
ways that I wasn't, you know,

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:49.350
I was trying to talk
about them in therapy.

00:20:49.350 --> 00:20:50.183
There were things like,

00:20:50.183 --> 00:20:52.560
and I know this is the
case for both of us,

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:55.200
in addition to MythiCon
and everything else

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:58.230
that we continually have
going on, just, you know,

00:20:58.230 --> 00:21:01.170
running Mythical and everything
else that we're doing.

00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:05.430
There was these special
sort of inaugural events,

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:09.318
in their own right, of me
performing as James and the Shame

00:21:09.318 --> 00:21:14.040
and you making your DJ debut
and doing the dance party.

00:21:14.040 --> 00:21:16.200
Kind of what we were calling
the bookends of MythiCon,

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:18.000
of me kind of opening
things up on night one

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:20.700
and you closing things down on night two.

00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:25.700
That I was very nervous
about because, you know,

00:21:27.570 --> 00:21:31.710
while I have performed music
in front of crowds for years,

00:21:31.710 --> 00:21:34.170
I've never done music like that.

00:21:34.170 --> 00:21:36.150
And I was performing my whole album,

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:39.240
which I had never
performed live for anyone.

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:40.800
And Jessie was joining me

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:42.990
and Jessie was super nervous

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:44.910
because she hasn't been
in front of an audience

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:45.990
in a very long time.

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:47.460
Right.

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:50.722
It was the new thing that
took a lot of your focus,

00:21:50.722 --> 00:21:55.722
you know, because it was,
that was a big deal, you know,

00:21:56.100 --> 00:21:58.950
and you hadn't done it before.

00:21:58.950 --> 00:22:01.192
Yeah, I definitely felt
that way about the DJ set.

00:22:01.192 --> 00:22:04.620
You know what, I have so much
that I want to talk about,

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:09.620
about my DJ premiere and some
things that happened afterward

00:22:10.770 --> 00:22:12.120
that I haven't told you about.

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:13.860
Like, there's so much, people are asking,

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:15.513
how did I come up with the name.

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:20.370
And, like, the entire
experience was so surreal.

00:22:20.370 --> 00:22:23.433
Like the costume, everything
that went into it,

00:22:24.270 --> 00:22:25.770
went into the persona.

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:29.820
I'd love to just table that
until, like, next episode.

00:22:29.820 --> 00:22:31.733
Because I have so much
that I want to unpack

00:22:31.733 --> 00:22:32.566
and tell you about.

00:22:32.566 --> 00:22:36.240
We can just have a ElkHound
SnuggleBaby unpacking

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:37.402
Episode.
Episode.

00:22:37.402 --> 00:22:38.940
Yeah, that sounds like a plan.

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:42.000
I have so much and I want,

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:43.560
there's so much I want to hear from you

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:45.240
that I haven't asked you about.

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:47.403
Like your vantage point
of the whole thing.

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:50.360
Oh, I had quite a vantage point.

00:22:50.360 --> 00:22:54.330
So next episode, we'll talk about the

00:22:54.330 --> 00:22:55.980
ElkHound SnuggleBaby of it all.

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:58.710
But yeah, I was talking to Jessie about,

00:22:58.710 --> 00:22:59.549
I was like, "You know what,

00:22:59.549 --> 00:23:02.400
I can help you be less nervous

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:04.200
because, like, you're
kind of doing something

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:06.300
that I have a point of
reference for, you know.

00:23:06.300 --> 00:23:08.340
In terms of, like, singing with Rhett,

00:23:08.340 --> 00:23:09.615
showing up for sound check,

00:23:09.615 --> 00:23:12.810
performing songs that you rarely perform".

00:23:12.810 --> 00:23:14.610
And then we didn't get to talk

00:23:14.610 --> 00:23:17.010
until we were, like, on the
way to the airport together.

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:17.843
And she's like,

00:23:17.843 --> 00:23:20.250
"Where is all this advice
you're gonna give me?".

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:21.930
And I thought I had
some really good advice.

00:23:21.930 --> 00:23:25.770
I was like, "First of all,
you're gonna be nervous".

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:28.890
Like, I don't get nervous
about a lot of things,

00:23:28.890 --> 00:23:30.990
the point you were making earlier,

00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:35.130
because you just have
to do something a lot

00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:36.690
to get to a point where
you're not nervous about it.

00:23:36.690 --> 00:23:39.834
Like, to do something big,

00:23:39.834 --> 00:23:43.110
that you've never done
before, in front of a crowd,

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:47.130
or maybe for you listening,
you're taking your own risk.

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:48.600
It might not be crowd oriented,

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:49.620
but it's something that you're, like,

00:23:49.620 --> 00:23:50.490
putting yourself out there.

00:23:50.490 --> 00:23:52.410
You're going out of your comfort zone.

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:54.870
Absolutely, you're gonna be nervous.

00:23:54.870 --> 00:23:58.260
And that's gonna keep happening
until you do it so much

00:23:58.260 --> 00:23:59.847
that you actually start,

00:23:59.847 --> 00:24:02.550
you need to look for something
else to make you nervous.

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:04.590
That's a great thing about
James and the Shame for you

00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:07.560
and the DJ set for me and MythiCon was,

00:24:07.560 --> 00:24:10.830
a lot of my emotional energy
was geared towards those things

00:24:10.830 --> 00:24:12.810
because they were so new.

00:24:12.810 --> 00:24:14.587
So I just, you know, I was like,

00:24:14.587 --> 00:24:16.050
"Yes, you're gonna feel nervous.

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:19.560
It's totally, it's part
of this, it's part of it.

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:22.956
Trying to find a way to
have some fun in the moment,

00:24:22.956 --> 00:24:27.270
is kind like a north
star. It may not happen".

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:28.627
But I was like,

00:24:28.627 --> 00:24:30.300
"The only practical advice I can give you

00:24:30.300 --> 00:24:31.860
is when you're singing,

00:24:31.860 --> 00:24:34.200
when you go up there and
you sing for the soundcheck,

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:37.650
don't worry about what any
crew or anybody thinks.

00:24:37.650 --> 00:24:39.780
Like, you're not trying
to sound good for anybody.

00:24:39.780 --> 00:24:41.610
So don't even worry about that".

00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:43.440
I used to be fixated on that.

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:46.410
Like, let's write a song
called the Soundcheck song

00:24:46.410 --> 00:24:49.350
so that we can not seem so
stupid out there for soundcheck.

00:24:49.350 --> 00:24:52.003
That's how self-conscious I
used to be about soundchecks.

00:24:52.003 --> 00:24:53.250
I was like, "Don't worry about that.

00:24:53.250 --> 00:24:55.140
This is about you getting comfortable.

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:57.030
And for me, when I sing with Rhett,

00:24:57.030 --> 00:24:58.740
and I'm on a mic and in a place

00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:00.640
that I don't know what it sounds like,

00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:04.170
I sing the melody, I sing in unison

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:05.610
before I break into any harmony,

00:25:05.610 --> 00:25:06.750
just so I can tell".

00:25:06.750 --> 00:25:07.657
And then I was like,

00:25:07.657 --> 00:25:10.440
"I thought this was, like,
gonna be the best advice ever".

00:25:10.440 --> 00:25:12.960
And she's like, "Yeah,
that's not gonna work for me.

00:25:12.960 --> 00:25:15.390
Like that would totally mess me up.

00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:17.490
I would totally lose my harmony line".

00:25:17.490 --> 00:25:19.980
Well, because like me,

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:22.893
because she is not a
typical harmony singer,

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:27.840
she has a tendency to
begin singing the melody.

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:29.430
The way that she screws up

00:25:29.430 --> 00:25:31.000
is by singing the melody by accident.

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:33.630
Well, that's a lot worse than going flat.

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:34.463
Anyway.

00:25:34.463 --> 00:25:35.910
So that was a big moment.

00:25:35.910 --> 00:25:37.058
So just to go to that, I mean,

00:25:37.058 --> 00:25:40.170
that was the first night,
we did the welcome.

00:25:40.170 --> 00:25:41.940
I think there's a lot of
things packed up in this

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:44.340
that kind of speak to MythiCon broadly.

00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:45.810
Yeah.

00:25:45.810 --> 00:25:50.070
So the first thing we did
when we got there on Thursday,

00:25:50.070 --> 00:25:52.080
so we arrived on Wednesday night,

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:56.670
but then Thursday mid-morning
we show up at Star Hill Ranch,

00:25:56.670 --> 00:25:58.020
is where we had it.

00:25:58.020 --> 00:26:02.280
And what we knew was
that, rain, first of all,

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:04.990
it had rained on Monday
pretty significantly

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:08.730
and it was supposed to
rain on Friday morning.

00:26:08.730 --> 00:26:11.463
So, everybody's arriving
at 3:00pm on Friday.

00:26:12.390 --> 00:26:14.910
The first, now Paisley.

00:26:14.910 --> 00:26:16.318
Nothing's paved here.

00:26:16.318 --> 00:26:18.180
It's a ranch.
Well, yeah.

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:19.080
Everything's dirt.

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:21.210
There's gravel and there's dirt.

00:26:21.210 --> 00:26:23.490
But so Paisley kind of gives us the tour

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:25.380
of the stuff that had already been set up.

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:27.570
There's still a lot of things
that were coming together,

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:28.410
you know, on that day,

00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:30.210
because people are getting
there, setting everything up,

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:31.470
but kind of takes us through,

00:26:31.470 --> 00:26:32.820
these are all these little,

00:26:32.820 --> 00:26:35.730
there's basically houses
and structures, buildings

00:26:35.730 --> 00:26:37.470
all along this thoroughfare.

00:26:37.470 --> 00:26:39.473
And each one had been
themed in a certain way.

00:26:39.473 --> 00:26:42.210
It was, you know, here's
the Mythical movie house

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:45.510
where they're constantly
running, you know, sets.

00:26:45.510 --> 00:26:46.680
Different programming about...

00:26:46.680 --> 00:26:49.350
Yeah, there's the Mythical Museum.

00:26:49.350 --> 00:26:50.183
Which was awesome!

00:26:50.183 --> 00:26:51.450
Which was super cool.

00:26:51.450 --> 00:26:53.640
The attention to detail
for that was awesome.

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:55.260
Kind of baked into the
meet and greet hall.

00:26:55.260 --> 00:26:57.720
There's the merchandise
area, there's the saloon.

00:26:57.720 --> 00:26:58.553
The VR experience.

00:26:58.553 --> 00:27:00.030
There's a VR experience,

00:27:00.030 --> 00:27:01.530
there's the Cotton Candy Randy House.

00:27:01.530 --> 00:27:03.360
Where you can, like, see
an accurate representation

00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:05.910
of the entire GMM set
and pick up everything

00:27:05.910 --> 00:27:08.910
and, like, hear us talking
in your ear about everything

00:27:08.910 --> 00:27:10.020
that you're interacting with

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:11.790
and darts that you're throwing.

00:27:11.790 --> 00:27:12.930
Yeah.

00:27:12.930 --> 00:27:14.640
So we took a tour of all that,

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:16.230
getting increasingly excited.

00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:17.647
Then we go down to the main stage.

00:27:17.647 --> 00:27:19.410
There's a barber shop!

00:27:19.410 --> 00:27:20.387
There's a barbershop, tattoo parlor.

00:27:20.387 --> 00:27:21.930
People were getting their hair cut,

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:23.426
their head shaved, their beard trimmed.

00:27:23.426 --> 00:27:27.480
Well, okay, there was a tattoo parlor

00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:30.330
and we did not anticipate

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:35.330
the literal several
people who wanted tattoos.

00:27:35.850 --> 00:27:36.750
Waiting for hours.

00:27:36.750 --> 00:27:39.275
So we actually had to...
To get an appointment.

00:27:39.275 --> 00:27:41.073
People got turned away because,

00:27:42.030 --> 00:27:43.890
it's one of those things that,

00:27:43.890 --> 00:27:45.450
we had it in mind from the beginning.

00:27:45.450 --> 00:27:47.970
We want to offer people tattoos,

00:27:47.970 --> 00:27:50.103
we want people to get tattoos at MythiCon.

00:27:51.210 --> 00:27:53.184
And we were like...

00:27:53.184 --> 00:27:54.180
Everybody wanted one, apparently.

00:27:54.180 --> 00:27:57.300
What percentage of like 2,300 people

00:27:57.300 --> 00:27:59.160
would want to get tattoos, right?

00:27:59.160 --> 00:28:00.185
Apparently 50%.

00:28:01.200 --> 00:28:02.850
More than you would think.

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:04.890
So anyway, so we're
getting excited about it,

00:28:04.890 --> 00:28:07.350
but then we go down to
the main stage area,

00:28:07.350 --> 00:28:09.930
which is an amphitheater,
sort of, you know,

00:28:09.930 --> 00:28:12.570
outdoor amphitheater, sort of grassy area.

00:28:12.570 --> 00:28:14.100
But the first thing we kind of notice

00:28:14.100 --> 00:28:18.603
is that there's a lot of
mud amongst this grass.

00:28:20.070 --> 00:28:21.570
I don't know the logistical history

00:28:21.570 --> 00:28:23.850
of exactly how we got to this point,

00:28:23.850 --> 00:28:26.430
but we were kind of alarmed

00:28:26.430 --> 00:28:29.100
and they were bringing in,
like, gravel, like, you know,

00:28:29.100 --> 00:28:30.630
crushed granite and stuff

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:31.917
and there were like trucks coming in

00:28:31.917 --> 00:28:33.760
and the guy who owns the place is, like,

00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:35.160
in a, you know...

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:35.993
Bobcat.

00:28:35.993 --> 00:28:37.660
A bobcat, like, moving stuff around.

00:28:38.550 --> 00:28:42.300
And we're like, okay, this
is a little disconcerting.

00:28:42.300 --> 00:28:43.920
They're like, "It's gonna rain on Friday,

00:28:43.920 --> 00:28:46.290
we're gonna cover the
whole thing with plastic,

00:28:46.290 --> 00:28:48.600
baseball field style".

00:28:48.600 --> 00:28:50.100
But getting a little nervous about that

00:28:50.100 --> 00:28:52.890
because that was where
three very important things

00:28:52.890 --> 00:28:54.420
were happening the first night,

00:28:54.420 --> 00:28:57.480
James and the Shame performance,
the live Ear Biscuit

00:28:57.480 --> 00:29:00.900
that we were gonna do on Saturday morning,

00:29:00.900 --> 00:29:03.930
and then of course the main
stage show on Saturday night.

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:04.800
And for each thing

00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:06.919
there was like a separate
stage presentation

00:29:06.919 --> 00:29:08.250
that we had prepared.

00:29:08.250 --> 00:29:09.083
Right.

00:29:09.083 --> 00:29:10.620
So that we weren't stealing
the thunder from our, like,

00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:12.480
main performance on Saturday night.

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:15.990
And it allowed everybody to
sit down in the amphitheater

00:29:15.990 --> 00:29:17.490
and, like, lounge.

00:29:17.490 --> 00:29:19.080
And it was kind of, like,

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:23.408
our vision was like picnic style hangout,

00:29:23.408 --> 00:29:27.390
a lot of space, and even
monitors setup I think.

00:29:27.390 --> 00:29:30.817
So it was like, it went from that to like,

00:29:30.817 --> 00:29:32.580
"Okay, we're just trying
to keep this thing

00:29:32.580 --> 00:29:34.050
as dry as possible".

00:29:34.050 --> 00:29:35.549
And there's no, we were like,

00:29:35.549 --> 00:29:36.660
"There ain't no way it's gonna happen".

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:39.360
You know, I'm a civil engineer by degree

00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:41.790
and at least by training
for a couple years.

00:29:41.790 --> 00:29:42.868
And drainage.

00:29:44.370 --> 00:29:47.197
Drainage and grading
and sort of, you know,

00:29:47.197 --> 00:29:50.250
pervious, you know, permeable surfaces.

00:29:50.250 --> 00:29:51.510
That was my specialty.

00:29:51.510 --> 00:29:52.410
So this is your fault.

00:29:52.410 --> 00:29:55.027
So, I actually, the
first thing I said was,

00:29:55.027 --> 00:29:57.630
"This is not, I don't
feel great about this.

00:29:57.630 --> 00:30:00.840
I don't feel great about
this being okay by Friday".

00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:02.490
The first thing I said when
I showed up on Thursday.

00:30:02.490 --> 00:30:04.109
But you weren't wearing a hard hat.

00:30:04.109 --> 00:30:04.942
So no one listened to you.

00:30:04.942 --> 00:30:05.775
Hard hat!

00:30:05.775 --> 00:30:10.775
So I, and this is really what represents,

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:14.040
we talked about this on stage at the show

00:30:14.040 --> 00:30:17.193
and also at the James and the Shame show.

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:22.410
This sort of captured, our
rehearsal on Thursday night,

00:30:22.410 --> 00:30:26.850
I think is a perfect
encapsulation of what Mythical is.

00:30:26.850 --> 00:30:29.550
And what Mythicality is
from a creative perspective.

00:30:29.550 --> 00:30:32.310
Because Jessie and I get up there

00:30:32.310 --> 00:30:36.390
to sing these songs
for the first time live

00:30:36.390 --> 00:30:41.220
and also for the first time
on an amplified system.

00:30:41.220 --> 00:30:42.810
Which, you know, there's
a difference between

00:30:42.810 --> 00:30:43.980
sitting down in your living room

00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:45.060
like Jessie and I have been practicing

00:30:45.060 --> 00:30:45.990
these songs in our living room.

00:30:45.990 --> 00:30:46.823
It's strange.

00:30:46.823 --> 00:30:48.390
That's what I was trying to tell her.

00:30:48.390 --> 00:30:50.490
And we were hoping that we would have time

00:30:50.490 --> 00:30:52.710
to maybe play a show somewhere in LA

00:30:52.710 --> 00:30:54.781
on, like, an amplified system.

00:30:54.781 --> 00:30:56.640
Of course we don't have time for that.

00:30:56.640 --> 00:30:58.830
So the day approaches,
it's like, here we go.

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:00.720
So the first thing I
noticed as I started singing

00:31:00.720 --> 00:31:02.490
into this giant sound system

00:31:02.490 --> 00:31:04.500
that's being broadcast
through the whole place

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:06.300
is that I sound horrible.

00:31:06.300 --> 00:31:08.040
That's the first thing you think

00:31:08.040 --> 00:31:11.880
because your ear has to tune into the way

00:31:11.880 --> 00:31:15.090
that everything is happening
and the monitors and the PA.

00:31:15.090 --> 00:31:15.923
You practiced.

00:31:15.923 --> 00:31:17.370
You did that rehearsal
The whole album.

00:31:17.370 --> 00:31:18.270
For an hour.

00:31:18.270 --> 00:31:21.570
I played the whole album
and I played the songs,

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:22.950
Jessie performed on three songs,

00:31:22.950 --> 00:31:24.391
we played those songs at least

00:31:24.391 --> 00:31:26.611
Multiple times.
three times each.

00:31:26.611 --> 00:31:29.790
Until the point where I'm
like, "Okay, I feel good".

00:31:29.790 --> 00:31:31.590
So Jessie leaves.

00:31:31.590 --> 00:31:36.590
And then you walk out of
a building and come down

00:31:36.660 --> 00:31:39.210
and without, like, changing
clothes or anything,

00:31:39.210 --> 00:31:42.600
we immediately go into the
main stage show rehearsal.

00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:45.300
And at that point is when
you can kind of see, like,

00:31:45.300 --> 00:31:48.270
these confused looks on people's faces.

00:31:48.270 --> 00:31:50.490
Because here I'm up there playing these

00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:54.990
serious country songs about
religious deconstruction,

00:31:54.990 --> 00:31:59.160
which is so out of the blue
and then all of a sudden.

00:31:59.160 --> 00:32:01.260
And all the crew and
people who are just hired

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:02.093
to make everything happen.

00:32:02.093 --> 00:32:03.193
They don't know what they're there for.

00:32:03.193 --> 00:32:05.223
They're just like, okay,
I can understand this.

00:32:05.223 --> 00:32:07.140
Then the next thing you know
He's a country artist?

00:32:07.140 --> 00:32:08.310
Another guy shows up

00:32:08.310 --> 00:32:11.730
and we're suddenly riding
on a double bicycle,

00:32:11.730 --> 00:32:13.880
rehearsing our route up onto stage.

00:32:13.880 --> 00:32:14.713
Right.

00:32:14.713 --> 00:32:15.651
At which point we rap,

00:32:15.651 --> 00:32:18.141
♪ Everything's okay ♪

00:32:18.141 --> 00:32:20.041
♪ Cause I'm on vacation ♪

00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:24.270
And at that point you
see people leaning over

00:32:24.270 --> 00:32:26.130
and talking to each other and actually,

00:32:26.130 --> 00:32:29.430
after that rehearsal that
night, I walked past a guy

00:32:29.430 --> 00:32:31.680
and he was trying to
explain what was going on

00:32:31.680 --> 00:32:34.053
and I heard little bits and pieces like,

00:32:35.189 --> 00:32:40.189
"They do YouTube and they've got fans

00:32:40.290 --> 00:32:42.690
that are showing up here".

00:32:42.690 --> 00:32:43.523
Yeah.

00:32:43.523 --> 00:32:44.850
And so, the fact that...

00:32:44.850 --> 00:32:46.260
Other than that I can't help you.

00:32:46.260 --> 00:32:47.640
And then if they stuck around

00:32:47.640 --> 00:32:49.950
for the ElkHound SnuggleBaby reveal,

00:32:49.950 --> 00:32:52.110
it's just the same guy that you saw,

00:32:52.110 --> 00:32:54.933
now in this outfit doing this.

00:32:56.010 --> 00:32:56.843
Yeah.

00:32:56.843 --> 00:32:58.560
But the thing I told the crowd

00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:00.300
at the James and the Shame show,

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:02.880
and maybe at the main show,

00:33:02.880 --> 00:33:04.560
that's the beauty of Mythicality.

00:33:04.560 --> 00:33:06.630
That's the beauty of Mythical.

00:33:06.630 --> 00:33:08.280
We're well rounded individuals,

00:33:09.211 --> 00:33:10.321
you never know what you're gonna get.

00:33:10.321 --> 00:33:13.230
Because, to everyone who was there,

00:33:13.230 --> 00:33:15.630
it makes complete sense

00:33:15.630 --> 00:33:17.910
that there's this guy and his wife

00:33:17.910 --> 00:33:21.210
singing these serious
country songs and then...

00:33:21.210 --> 00:33:22.680
That are making people weep.

00:33:22.680 --> 00:33:24.810
Yeah, and listen, I'll tell you about

00:33:24.810 --> 00:33:27.810
how I got through that set
without crying in a moment.

00:33:27.810 --> 00:33:32.810
But then, he and this other
guy are doing this crazy show

00:33:34.050 --> 00:33:36.870
where they summon an alien
and we'll get into that.

00:33:36.870 --> 00:33:39.273
And then it follows up with the other guy,

00:33:40.140 --> 00:33:41.490
who joined him on stage,

00:33:41.490 --> 00:33:46.490
is now in this outfit
that I'll let you describe

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:48.496
at a later point.

00:33:48.496 --> 00:33:49.329
Thank you.

00:33:49.329 --> 00:33:51.330
He calls himself ElkHound SnuggleBaby

00:33:51.330 --> 00:33:52.983
and he's DJing a party.

00:33:54.180 --> 00:33:56.793
To any outsider, it makes no sense at all.

00:33:57.900 --> 00:33:59.587
But to a Mythical Beast, it's like,

00:33:59.587 --> 00:34:03.330
"Yeah, of course, this
completely makes sense.

00:34:03.330 --> 00:34:05.105
It all fits together in this world".

00:34:05.105 --> 00:34:05.938
I loved it.

00:34:05.938 --> 00:34:06.780
And I love the fact...
I love it so much.

00:34:06.780 --> 00:34:07.740
that those people get it.

00:34:07.740 --> 00:34:10.290
And that's why we did it,
because they do get it.

00:34:10.290 --> 00:34:12.097
And that's what I told Jessie, I said,

00:34:12.097 --> 00:34:14.250
"The reason you shouldn't be nervous

00:34:14.250 --> 00:34:19.250
is because this audience
will be the warmest audience

00:34:21.930 --> 00:34:23.472
and most supportive audience that

00:34:23.472 --> 00:34:25.650
you could possibly imagine".
Oh yeah.

00:34:25.650 --> 00:34:28.470
So much so, that when she comes out,

00:34:28.470 --> 00:34:31.020
they go nuts when she comes out.

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:33.780
And she was trying not to cry.

00:34:33.780 --> 00:34:34.980
She was, you could see it.

00:34:34.980 --> 00:34:36.090
She was like trying not to cry.

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:36.967
And I was like, I was thinking,

00:34:36.967 --> 00:34:40.200
"I told you, I told you you
had nothing to worry about".

00:34:40.200 --> 00:34:41.850
Now it's impossible to tell
somebody to not be nervous.

00:34:41.850 --> 00:34:44.280
And she started singing and she was like

00:34:44.280 --> 00:34:45.483
No, she sounded great.

00:34:46.620 --> 00:34:47.610
Let me tell you man.

00:34:47.610 --> 00:34:50.247
So that was, just to
talk briefly about that

00:34:50.247 --> 00:34:51.180
and we can move on.

00:34:51.180 --> 00:34:53.130
Well, there was just the
one part of the story

00:34:53.130 --> 00:34:54.000
you can't leave out,

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:58.290
is that we're driving to
the venue Friday morning

00:34:58.290 --> 00:35:01.473
to then, like, get ready
for the whole thing.

00:35:01.473 --> 00:35:02.306
Yeah.

00:35:02.306 --> 00:35:05.527
And we get a phone call
from Stevie who's like,

00:35:05.527 --> 00:35:07.772
"I'm already here. We're moving.

00:35:07.772 --> 00:35:10.560
Nothing is on the amphitheater stage.

00:35:10.560 --> 00:35:13.830
Nothing except for your main show.

00:35:13.830 --> 00:35:16.687
No Ear Biscuits, no James
and the Shame tonight".

00:35:16.687 --> 00:35:18.210
"We're moving it all to the big tents".

00:35:18.210 --> 00:35:19.440
Because we have to do everything we can

00:35:19.440 --> 00:35:21.060
to try to preserve the grounds

00:35:21.060 --> 00:35:23.310
so that people can see
your main show there.

00:35:23.310 --> 00:35:25.950
So all of that practice
you did and acclimation,

00:35:25.950 --> 00:35:27.930
you got to that sound system.

00:35:27.930 --> 00:35:28.763
Forget all of that.

00:35:28.763 --> 00:35:29.940
Because now James and the Shame

00:35:29.940 --> 00:35:31.650
is performing in the big tent.

00:35:31.650 --> 00:35:32.640
And the big tent,

00:35:32.640 --> 00:35:34.710
the reason James and the
Shame and Ear Biscuits

00:35:34.710 --> 00:35:37.260
were not originally
scheduled for the big tent,

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:39.450
is because they're not big
enough for the amount of people

00:35:39.450 --> 00:35:41.640
that we anticipated being
at those two events.

00:35:41.640 --> 00:35:43.290
Right.
So...

00:35:43.290 --> 00:35:44.700
We made the best of it.

00:35:44.700 --> 00:35:46.230
Everybody had to stand.

00:35:46.230 --> 00:35:48.690
That wasn't ideal.
It wasn't ideal.

00:35:48.690 --> 00:35:49.770
From my perspective...
But you know,

00:35:49.770 --> 00:35:50.603
everybody made the best of it.

00:35:50.603 --> 00:35:52.500
From my perspective,

00:35:52.500 --> 00:35:54.242
I didn't let it affect me.

00:35:54.242 --> 00:35:55.267
Because I was like,

00:35:55.267 --> 00:35:57.007
"Of course, of course
I'm gonna have to..."

00:35:57.007 --> 00:35:57.840
You can't let it affect you.

00:35:57.840 --> 00:35:59.700
I spent all that time getting ready

00:35:59.700 --> 00:36:01.470
on this particular sound system

00:36:01.470 --> 00:36:03.180
and now we've gotta go do a sound check,

00:36:03.180 --> 00:36:05.880
a short sound check, on
this completely new system

00:36:05.880 --> 00:36:07.080
and just be ready for it.

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:10.030
So Jessie and I show up, we do
our sound check in the tent,

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:12.930
you know, completely different sound.

00:36:12.930 --> 00:36:15.963
It feels completely different
but readjusted pretty quickly.

00:36:17.340 --> 00:36:18.360
And so I was like, okay.

00:36:18.360 --> 00:36:21.300
And I was like, "I hate the
fact that people are going to,

00:36:21.300 --> 00:36:22.920
there's gonna be people who can't see

00:36:22.920 --> 00:36:24.750
because there's only so many
people that can fit in here

00:36:24.750 --> 00:36:26.040
and we've got these TVs"

00:36:26.040 --> 00:36:28.320
and we did what we could in a very-

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:30.030
Shout out to the Mythical team.

00:36:30.030 --> 00:36:33.240
And also Gary and Matt who, you know,

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:35.880
produced the show from
an acoustic standpoint

00:36:35.880 --> 00:36:37.560
and beyond that, you know,

00:36:37.560 --> 00:36:39.570
basically just made the technical aspects

00:36:39.570 --> 00:36:41.133
of the live events happen.

00:36:43.320 --> 00:36:44.763
Very quick adjustments.

00:36:46.740 --> 00:36:47.880
But that was unfortunate because

00:36:47.880 --> 00:36:49.560
people did have to stand
and some people couldn't see

00:36:49.560 --> 00:36:51.630
and it just wasn't planned,
it wasn't how wanted to,

00:36:51.630 --> 00:36:52.560
it was inaudible.

00:36:52.560 --> 00:36:53.850
The energy was great though

00:36:53.850 --> 00:36:55.830
because everybody was so jammed in there.

00:36:55.830 --> 00:36:58.500
And, like, at the beginning of your set,

00:36:58.500 --> 00:37:01.230
from my perspective, I was like, you know,

00:37:01.230 --> 00:37:03.360
we kinda had to hide.

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:07.860
Like, I was literally looking
through a crack in the tent

00:37:07.860 --> 00:37:09.120
watching your set.

00:37:09.120 --> 00:37:12.060
And then later on, they
found a space for us

00:37:12.060 --> 00:37:15.360
to go up to the front, like,
be right next to the stage,

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:16.530
which ended up working out.

00:37:16.530 --> 00:37:18.990
But, like, even from that vantage point

00:37:18.990 --> 00:37:20.913
of like looking through a crack,

00:37:23.037 --> 00:37:25.039
it was just, it was electric.

00:37:25.039 --> 00:37:28.380
The heat, the body heat and smells

00:37:28.380 --> 00:37:29.460
that were coming out of that tent.

00:37:29.460 --> 00:37:31.320
I was like, man, everybody's excited.

00:37:31.320 --> 00:37:33.630
Well, especially at the final party.

00:37:33.630 --> 00:37:34.463
Oh God.

00:37:34.463 --> 00:37:35.940
We can talk about that next time.

00:37:35.940 --> 00:37:38.783
So it was a wonderful experience.

00:37:38.783 --> 00:37:42.300
I mean, I said it on Twitter the next day.

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:44.340
One of the most special nights of my life.

00:37:44.340 --> 00:37:45.640
I completely believe that,

00:37:45.640 --> 00:37:48.270
the whole thing was special.

00:37:48.270 --> 00:37:50.430
That performance because it was

00:37:50.430 --> 00:37:55.140
being able to sing these
songs to a group of people

00:37:55.140 --> 00:37:56.520
who, you know, I didn't know.

00:37:56.520 --> 00:37:58.380
You never know, like,
how many of these people

00:37:58.380 --> 00:38:01.023
who are Mythical Beasts
are listening to the songs.

00:38:01.860 --> 00:38:03.180
I don't know.

00:38:03.180 --> 00:38:04.560
But when I start singing

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:06.240
and they immediately start singing along.

00:38:06.240 --> 00:38:07.140
That's awesome.

00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:10.620
And so I was closing my eyes for

00:38:10.620 --> 00:38:11.670
pretty much the whole time.

00:38:11.670 --> 00:38:13.620
You may have noticed if
you watched me perform.

00:38:13.620 --> 00:38:14.610
For two reasons, number one,

00:38:14.610 --> 00:38:16.380
because I was trying
to remember the lyrics.

00:38:16.380 --> 00:38:19.350
Because when you haven't
performed these songs live,

00:38:19.350 --> 00:38:21.750
it's another part of your
brain that you have to turn on

00:38:21.750 --> 00:38:24.060
in order to remember 11 songs in a row.

00:38:24.060 --> 00:38:24.893
Yeah.

00:38:24.893 --> 00:38:25.740
Chords and lyrics.

00:38:25.740 --> 00:38:28.050
And also try to stay on tune.

00:38:28.050 --> 00:38:29.760
And also trying not to cry.

00:38:29.760 --> 00:38:30.593
Because I was like,

00:38:30.593 --> 00:38:33.720
"If I really take in
what's happening right now

00:38:33.720 --> 00:38:36.000
and the way that these
people are being supportive"

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:39.720
and God knows if I look at
anybody on the front row,

00:38:39.720 --> 00:38:42.210
every time I kind of peeked
down there, people are weeping.

00:38:42.210 --> 00:38:45.000
I'm like, so if I did open
my eyes, I looked above them,

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:47.220
like my dad taught me to
do in public speaking.

00:38:47.220 --> 00:38:50.220
You gotta look above the crowd,
so you don't get distracted.

00:38:51.600 --> 00:38:53.070
Just the feedback from people

00:38:53.070 --> 00:38:55.140
in all of our meet and greets.

00:38:55.140 --> 00:38:56.382
By this point, we had already done

00:38:56.382 --> 00:38:58.290
one, two hour meet and greet.

00:38:58.290 --> 00:38:59.220
Right before, yeah.

00:38:59.220 --> 00:39:00.570
That was before our welcome.

00:39:00.570 --> 00:39:02.160
So it was kind of cool to, like,

00:39:02.160 --> 00:39:04.470
because we couldn't walk around.

00:39:04.470 --> 00:39:06.330
It was kind of cool to
have that meet and greet

00:39:06.330 --> 00:39:11.330
and to hear from fans and
their first reactions.

00:39:11.610 --> 00:39:13.410
But then the second meet and
greet, on the second day,

00:39:13.410 --> 00:39:15.960
is when people were able to talk to you

00:39:15.960 --> 00:39:19.470
about the James and the Shame experience.

00:39:19.470 --> 00:39:21.780
And people just had so many

00:39:21.780 --> 00:39:24.360
heartwarming, moving things to say

00:39:24.360 --> 00:39:27.660
about our deconstruction stories,

00:39:27.660 --> 00:39:29.010
their identification with it,

00:39:29.010 --> 00:39:30.960
even if they ended up
in a different place.

00:39:30.960 --> 00:39:31.910
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:39:32.940 --> 00:39:34.110
They were grateful for it.

00:39:34.110 --> 00:39:36.570
And then the people who
connected so much with the music,

00:39:36.570 --> 00:39:41.126
it's like, your album has
become this, in their own words,

00:39:41.126 --> 00:39:43.173
like a lifeline.

00:39:44.250 --> 00:39:46.500
A lifeline, like, something
they can hold onto

00:39:46.500 --> 00:39:50.370
that's, like, they have this
shared experience in music

00:39:50.370 --> 00:39:53.730
that, like, a lot of
couples were talking about

00:39:53.730 --> 00:39:55.620
how they connect over this album

00:39:55.620 --> 00:39:58.410
because it, a lot of
people were saying how,

00:39:58.410 --> 00:40:01.980
it expressed the things that
they couldn't put into words,

00:40:01.980 --> 00:40:03.540
much less music.

00:40:03.540 --> 00:40:05.377
And I was really moved to just, you know,

00:40:05.377 --> 00:40:08.520
just to witness everybody come up

00:40:08.520 --> 00:40:10.230
and say how much they connected with that.

00:40:10.230 --> 00:40:11.850
Obviously, it was happening
during the concert,

00:40:11.850 --> 00:40:14.580
but, like, the stuff
they had to say after...

00:40:14.580 --> 00:40:16.003
It was very moving.
So special.

00:40:16.003 --> 00:40:18.720
And it was so special for Jessie too.

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:20.310
I mean, first of all,

00:40:20.310 --> 00:40:22.380
we almost didn't perform 'Kill a Man'

00:40:22.380 --> 00:40:23.880
because it's so guitar driven

00:40:23.880 --> 00:40:25.140
and I didn't wanna sell it short

00:40:25.140 --> 00:40:26.370
by just doing an acoustic version,

00:40:26.370 --> 00:40:29.010
but we put an acoustic version together.

00:40:29.010 --> 00:40:30.210
I'm so glad we did it

00:40:30.210 --> 00:40:32.370
because it was the
highlight of the concert.

00:40:32.370 --> 00:40:34.140
Because Jessie, you know,

00:40:34.140 --> 00:40:35.100
which a lot of people didn't know,

00:40:35.100 --> 00:40:37.680
that she's the one singing the opera

00:40:37.680 --> 00:40:39.628
on that part of 'Kill a Man'.

00:40:39.628 --> 00:40:40.461
Yeah.

00:40:40.461 --> 00:40:42.300
And she did it and...

00:40:42.300 --> 00:40:43.170
Jaw's were dropping.

00:40:43.170 --> 00:40:44.340
She brought the house down.

00:40:44.340 --> 00:40:45.262
Yeah, it was crazy.

00:40:45.262 --> 00:40:48.232
The length of applause
when she was done with it,

00:40:48.232 --> 00:40:50.520
I was like, "I don't know how
long this is gonna go on".

00:40:50.520 --> 00:40:51.353
Oh yeah.

00:40:51.353 --> 00:40:52.186
It blew people's minds.
She felt so good.

00:40:52.186 --> 00:40:54.621
It was such a good experience
for her so much so,

00:40:54.621 --> 00:40:56.137
you know, where she was like,

00:40:56.137 --> 00:40:58.230
"Okay, I feel like if we
have to do that again,

00:40:58.230 --> 00:40:59.520
I can think about it in a way

00:40:59.520 --> 00:41:03.390
that actually I can anticipate
it in a positive way".

00:41:03.390 --> 00:41:04.440
I will take this,

00:41:04.440 --> 00:41:06.450
just to quickly move on
from James and the Shame,

00:41:06.450 --> 00:41:08.370
I did wanna say, because I
don't wanna forget about it.

00:41:08.370 --> 00:41:10.740
I announced it at MythiCon,

00:41:10.740 --> 00:41:13.320
and I'm gonna announce it right here,

00:41:13.320 --> 00:41:16.620
that a lot of people asked
about having a physical album

00:41:16.620 --> 00:41:17.730
for 'Human Overboard'.

00:41:17.730 --> 00:41:21.363
And so, that is on sale right now.

00:41:22.770 --> 00:41:24.630
We're gonna have, sort
of, a pre-sale window.

00:41:24.630 --> 00:41:29.130
I'm selling a record and yes,
also a CD and a cassette,

00:41:29.130 --> 00:41:31.530
because people, we were
selling smaller quantities

00:41:31.530 --> 00:41:32.580
of the CD and the cassette,

00:41:32.580 --> 00:41:35.730
but there was was enough
people who asked about it.

00:41:35.730 --> 00:41:36.563
So it's a pre-order.

00:41:36.563 --> 00:41:38.760
You gotta like, say,
you gotta make an order

00:41:38.760 --> 00:41:40.140
and then that way you
know how many to make.

00:41:40.140 --> 00:41:41.730
Yeah, and you know,

00:41:41.730 --> 00:41:43.530
you're gonna have to
be patient, obviously,

00:41:43.530 --> 00:41:46.170
because vinyl production
is very slow right now,

00:41:46.170 --> 00:41:48.360
but there's a pre-order window.

00:41:48.360 --> 00:41:50.010
We've got a really cool design.

00:41:50.010 --> 00:41:54.960
We're kind of taking, being
inspired by the album art

00:41:54.960 --> 00:41:57.240
to actually make a record that has this

00:41:57.240 --> 00:41:59.370
cool, like, color splash.

00:41:59.370 --> 00:42:00.870
You know, we're not just
doing a black record.

00:42:00.870 --> 00:42:05.106
It's this blue and green, sort
of, tie dye color mix thing

00:42:05.106 --> 00:42:08.190
that's happening on the record.

00:42:08.190 --> 00:42:11.130
Really nice interior sleeve with lyrics.

00:42:11.130 --> 00:42:13.428
And then the CD's probably
gonna have this, like,

00:42:13.428 --> 00:42:16.529
poster fold inside, it's got lyrics on it

00:42:16.529 --> 00:42:17.679
and that kind of thing.

00:42:18.630 --> 00:42:21.450
And also there's some
exclusive merch that's being,

00:42:21.450 --> 00:42:24.180
you can bundle with it, or you can get it,

00:42:24.180 --> 00:42:25.770
just kind of as part of the

00:42:25.770 --> 00:42:28.113
'Human Overboard' physical album.

00:42:29.580 --> 00:42:31.380
Got a t-shirt that has, like,

00:42:31.380 --> 00:42:33.720
a cool bluegreen, kind of, tie dye pattern

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:36.150
that's coming off of like
the ocean inspiration

00:42:36.150 --> 00:42:38.460
from the album cover and even a sweatshirt

00:42:38.460 --> 00:42:42.000
with lyrics from 'Give A Damn' on it.

00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:43.539
So, you can bundle that together,

00:42:43.539 --> 00:42:45.720
you can get them individually.

00:42:45.720 --> 00:42:47.520
Again, jamesandtheshame.com.

00:42:47.520 --> 00:42:50.430
This stuff's not gonna
be available for long.

00:42:50.430 --> 00:42:55.230
And also the first few
hundred or so copies

00:42:55.230 --> 00:42:57.090
of the record that I sell,

00:42:57.090 --> 00:43:00.930
I'm gonna have a signed exclusive postcard

00:43:00.930 --> 00:43:02.130
that goes inside those,

00:43:02.130 --> 00:43:05.100
so it comes with a signature as well.

00:43:05.100 --> 00:43:08.040
So jamesandtheshame.com,
appreciate your support.

00:43:08.040 --> 00:43:11.820
You know, we were well taken
care of in terms of planning,

00:43:11.820 --> 00:43:14.280
you know, even when it
comes to like security,

00:43:14.280 --> 00:43:16.503
that was a big unknown.

00:43:17.550 --> 00:43:22.080
When you hire an event production company

00:43:22.080 --> 00:43:22.913
to work on something,

00:43:22.913 --> 00:43:25.140
they don't know our fans
and it's like, okay,

00:43:25.140 --> 00:43:28.230
as part of this you gotta
have security detail.

00:43:28.230 --> 00:43:31.050
You gotta make sure that,
you know, for any event,

00:43:31.050 --> 00:43:34.170
you want everybody attending to feel safe.

00:43:34.170 --> 00:43:39.170
And then also as, like,
talent on this thing,

00:43:39.810 --> 00:43:41.430
they wanted us to feel safe.

00:43:41.430 --> 00:43:43.320
So, like, there was security

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:46.800
that were assigned to
different things, including us.

00:43:46.800 --> 00:43:49.710
And, you know what I'll say,

00:43:49.710 --> 00:43:52.470
the Mythical Beasts
were, like, super chill.

00:43:52.470 --> 00:43:55.980
It was like, it felt in a lot of ways like

00:43:55.980 --> 00:43:58.170
a family reunion, you know,

00:43:58.170 --> 00:44:00.330
it's just like some
people wanted pictures,

00:44:00.330 --> 00:44:02.760
a lot of people just wanted
to give a friendly hello,

00:44:02.760 --> 00:44:04.350
you know, if they happened to see crew,

00:44:04.350 --> 00:44:07.650
the crew had great experience
in interacting with fans.

00:44:07.650 --> 00:44:12.270
And so, you know, credit
to Mythical Beasts

00:44:12.270 --> 00:44:17.190
and how grounded they were.

00:44:17.190 --> 00:44:19.170
So many people came up.

00:44:19.170 --> 00:44:22.590
And we've heard this before
when we do, when we tour,

00:44:22.590 --> 00:44:24.379
people, the venues are like,

00:44:24.379 --> 00:44:26.670
"How are your fans so cooperative?

00:44:26.670 --> 00:44:27.690
And they don't cause trouble

00:44:27.690 --> 00:44:30.750
and they're not rowdy
and they're not rude".

00:44:30.750 --> 00:44:33.257
That was what everybody
at Star Hill was saying.

00:44:33.257 --> 00:44:34.090
Yeah.

00:44:34.090 --> 00:44:34.923
They were talking about, they were like,

00:44:34.923 --> 00:44:36.810
"They don't litter".

00:44:36.810 --> 00:44:39.933
And then when somebody, like,
they were telling a story,

00:44:40.830 --> 00:44:43.300
there was a time when
somebody took a trash can,

00:44:43.300 --> 00:44:46.740
a trash bag out of a trash
can, in order to throw it away.

00:44:46.740 --> 00:44:47.640
And there's that moment

00:44:47.640 --> 00:44:49.170
where you don't have a trash bag in there.

00:44:49.170 --> 00:44:50.220
Yeah.

00:44:50.220 --> 00:44:53.370
And, like, one of the
fans came up to somebody

00:44:53.370 --> 00:44:54.203
who worked there and said,

00:44:54.203 --> 00:44:56.637
"We need to get a bag in
here so people, you know".

00:44:58.080 --> 00:44:58.950
I love that.

00:44:58.950 --> 00:44:59.970
They didn't have their own bag.

00:44:59.970 --> 00:45:01.508
That's the community.

00:45:01.508 --> 00:45:02.670
I would have had my own bag.

00:45:02.670 --> 00:45:05.426
That's the community that
we together have cultivated,

00:45:05.426 --> 00:45:08.310
is a group of good people.

00:45:08.310 --> 00:45:09.143
But.

00:45:09.143 --> 00:45:09.976
I love it.

00:45:09.976 --> 00:45:13.200
I will, I do have to share this one story.

00:45:13.200 --> 00:45:15.930
I mean, there's an exception to every rule

00:45:15.930 --> 00:45:20.040
and I didn't tell you ahead of time,

00:45:20.040 --> 00:45:23.190
but I'm just gonna share
it, it doesn't matter.

00:45:23.190 --> 00:45:26.690
So, the security that we had, they were,

00:45:30.390 --> 00:45:31.770
yeah, they were taking care of us.

00:45:31.770 --> 00:45:33.390
So, you know, I felt safe,

00:45:33.390 --> 00:45:37.680
but like Christie told
me at a certain point

00:45:37.680 --> 00:45:39.783
that, like, as she was,

00:45:41.130 --> 00:45:42.930
I think it was the James
and the Shame concert,

00:45:42.930 --> 00:45:45.300
because they got a place,

00:45:45.300 --> 00:45:47.073
they were ushered to a place, like,

00:45:49.320 --> 00:45:53.130
Christie and the kids,
behind the sound area.

00:45:53.130 --> 00:45:54.540
And then there was somebody

00:45:54.540 --> 00:45:57.150
who was making her pretty uncomfortable

00:45:57.150 --> 00:45:59.820
because they just kept staring at her.

00:45:59.820 --> 00:46:03.510
And, like, and then she
would look over her shoulder

00:46:03.510 --> 00:46:06.210
and this guy was staring at her

00:46:06.210 --> 00:46:11.160
and then she started looking more often.

00:46:11.160 --> 00:46:11.993
And I was like,

00:46:11.993 --> 00:46:13.290
"Well he probably thought
you were interested".

00:46:13.290 --> 00:46:17.460
She was like, "Every time I
looked back he was closer".

00:46:17.460 --> 00:46:19.800
And she said he had like shifty eyes,

00:46:19.800 --> 00:46:21.810
kinda like, looking at
her and looking around.

00:46:21.810 --> 00:46:22.920
So then she called...

00:46:22.920 --> 00:46:24.815
Didn't seem to be enjoying himself.

00:46:24.815 --> 00:46:28.770
Uhuh. And then so she, but
she did the right thing

00:46:28.770 --> 00:46:32.310
and she got someone to contact security

00:46:32.310 --> 00:46:33.967
and reported this guy and said,

00:46:33.967 --> 00:46:37.140
"Okay, there's somebody here

00:46:37.140 --> 00:46:38.310
who's making me really uncomfortable.

00:46:38.310 --> 00:46:41.160
He's staring at me, he's not
happy, he's getting closer".

00:46:43.290 --> 00:46:46.320
And the security said,
"We'll get right on it".

00:46:46.320 --> 00:46:51.320
And then the guy who was staring at her

00:46:52.710 --> 00:46:54.975
turned out to be the head of security.

00:46:56.580 --> 00:46:58.565
Yeah, this is such a Christie story.

00:47:00.090 --> 00:47:01.470
It's like, we're gonna call

00:47:01.470 --> 00:47:02.430
the head of security right now.

00:47:02.430 --> 00:47:03.300
And then all of a sudden the person

00:47:03.300 --> 00:47:04.907
that's staring at Christie's
answering the phone

00:47:04.907 --> 00:47:07.260
like, "Yeah, I got you".

00:47:07.260 --> 00:47:08.970
You know what, I wonder how that was,

00:47:08.970 --> 00:47:11.647
it was just like, "Could
you describe him?".

00:47:11.647 --> 00:47:13.677
"He's got on a black jacket".

00:47:14.568 --> 00:47:15.427
"He's got an earpiece in".

00:47:15.427 --> 00:47:20.427
"He's bearded with gray hair, khakis".

00:47:20.820 --> 00:47:22.085
She turned in the head
of security to security.

00:47:22.085 --> 00:47:24.510
"He's currently talking on the phone"

00:47:24.510 --> 00:47:26.693
Yeah, and they were great.

00:47:27.563 --> 00:47:29.100
I mean, I'm like clueless.

00:47:29.100 --> 00:47:31.440
I'm like, "Oh yeah, like
there's eight security".

00:47:31.440 --> 00:47:34.230
And again, not that the Mythical Beasts

00:47:34.230 --> 00:47:36.810
are to be worried about,

00:47:36.810 --> 00:47:39.510
but again, it's standard protocol.

00:47:39.510 --> 00:47:40.743
You gotta make sure that,

00:47:40.743 --> 00:47:42.652
that everybody from Mythical

00:47:42.652 --> 00:47:45.450
and everybody in our family is protected

00:47:45.450 --> 00:47:47.100
from somebody who might
want to do something.

00:47:47.100 --> 00:47:48.390
So I think that...

00:47:48.390 --> 00:47:49.223
And we didn't get to...

00:47:49.223 --> 00:47:50.190
They were great.
Yeah, they were great.

00:47:50.190 --> 00:47:52.020
We didn't get to see everything,

00:47:52.020 --> 00:47:53.010
like all the stuff that,

00:47:53.010 --> 00:47:54.810
all the other podcasts that went on,

00:47:54.810 --> 00:47:57.270
like we could hear it from the stage.

00:47:57.270 --> 00:48:00.360
And then, like, the
Mythical Kitchen, like,

00:48:00.360 --> 00:48:02.460
baby food eating contest.

00:48:02.460 --> 00:48:04.770
I saw Josh in a diaper beforehand

00:48:04.770 --> 00:48:07.470
and I saw that he still
had his belt afterward.

00:48:07.470 --> 00:48:08.702
But we didn't get to
see a lot of that stuff.

00:48:08.702 --> 00:48:09.535
He defended his title.

00:48:09.535 --> 00:48:13.050
But everything that we
did get to experience

00:48:13.050 --> 00:48:14.460
vicariously through fans

00:48:14.460 --> 00:48:16.470
and as they talked to us
in the meet and greets

00:48:16.470 --> 00:48:19.050
or the stuff that we would
see every now and again

00:48:19.050 --> 00:48:21.437
and all the stuff that we participated in,

00:48:21.437 --> 00:48:24.150
it was still a very
fulfilling experience for us.

00:48:24.150 --> 00:48:26.370
Like getting up there the next day,

00:48:26.370 --> 00:48:28.929
my dad and I did a live dispatches

00:48:28.929 --> 00:48:33.929
and I was just, just
grinning from ear to ear

00:48:33.930 --> 00:48:37.290
watching him take in
all this fan adulation

00:48:37.290 --> 00:48:38.130
for the first time.

00:48:38.130 --> 00:48:39.660
It was so special.

00:48:39.660 --> 00:48:42.556
Like, just watching his
experience the whole time.

00:48:42.556 --> 00:48:45.120
It was like bringing
your kid to Disneyland

00:48:45.120 --> 00:48:46.470
for the first time

00:48:46.470 --> 00:48:49.380
and everybody knew your kid at Disneyland.

00:48:49.380 --> 00:48:50.537
It's like, that's what it felt like.

00:48:50.537 --> 00:48:52.290
But it goes a step beyond that

00:48:52.290 --> 00:48:55.410
because it's as if your
dad has been preparing

00:48:55.410 --> 00:48:56.940
for this moment his entire life.

00:48:56.940 --> 00:48:58.770
He didn't seem nervous.

00:48:58.770 --> 00:49:01.050
And it's not just that
he doesn't seem nervous.

00:49:01.050 --> 00:49:02.580
And, you know, because people...

00:49:02.580 --> 00:49:03.660
He was like "Finally!".

00:49:03.660 --> 00:49:05.370
People in entertainment get their

00:49:05.370 --> 00:49:07.380
parents involved for different reasons

00:49:07.380 --> 00:49:09.780
because your parents
tend to be entertaining,

00:49:09.780 --> 00:49:11.980
not necessarily as
entertaining as your dad.

00:49:12.990 --> 00:49:15.600
But he's not just this
willing participant.

00:49:15.600 --> 00:49:17.880
He's more than that.

00:49:17.880 --> 00:49:18.713
He's eager.

00:49:18.713 --> 00:49:21.180
He's loving every minute of it.

00:49:21.180 --> 00:49:23.070
And the other thing that he's done,

00:49:23.070 --> 00:49:25.773
which he did multiple times,

00:49:27.210 --> 00:49:29.220
but especially at the end

00:49:29.220 --> 00:49:31.260
when we were with the
crew after the brunch

00:49:31.260 --> 00:49:32.430
and the crew, basically,

00:49:32.430 --> 00:49:35.310
this was, like, us
signing off of MythiCon.

00:49:35.310 --> 00:49:36.143
Yeah.

00:49:36.143 --> 00:49:37.047
And it's just the Mythical crew

00:49:37.047 --> 00:49:40.440
who had worked MythiCon,
kind of, together in a room.

00:49:40.440 --> 00:49:43.110
He did this several times,
but he did it there as well.

00:49:43.110 --> 00:49:46.893
He's like, this is my
opportunity to say something.

00:49:46.893 --> 00:49:47.726
Yeah.

00:49:47.726 --> 00:49:48.780
And so he gets everybody's attention

00:49:48.780 --> 00:49:50.850
and he says this really sweet thing

00:49:50.850 --> 00:49:52.680
about how welcome he feels.

00:49:52.680 --> 00:49:54.210
And I looked at Nancy

00:49:54.210 --> 00:49:55.486
and Nancy's tearing up as he's talking.

00:49:55.486 --> 00:49:56.807
I know!

00:49:56.807 --> 00:50:00.990
Nancy, his wife, your
stepmother is what she is.

00:50:00.990 --> 00:50:01.823
I know you don't...

00:50:01.823 --> 00:50:02.656
Yeah, technically.
Consider her that,

00:50:02.656 --> 00:50:03.489
but that's what she is.

00:50:03.489 --> 00:50:04.620
Technically, yeah.

00:50:04.620 --> 00:50:06.283
They just had the best time.

00:50:06.283 --> 00:50:07.116
It was so sweet.

00:50:07.116 --> 00:50:09.210
And they kept talking about the fans,

00:50:09.210 --> 00:50:10.680
the people that they were meeting.

00:50:10.680 --> 00:50:12.990
Because you have an idea, as a parent,

00:50:12.990 --> 00:50:17.340
you have an idea of what
it is that your child does,

00:50:17.340 --> 00:50:18.630
but not really.

00:50:18.630 --> 00:50:19.463
Not really.

00:50:19.463 --> 00:50:20.296
Right.

00:50:20.296 --> 00:50:21.129
And you can talk about it.

00:50:21.129 --> 00:50:21.962
You can be like, yeah.

00:50:21.962 --> 00:50:22.795
It's like, there are these people

00:50:22.795 --> 00:50:25.170
who have identified with the things that

00:50:25.170 --> 00:50:28.980
we and our company have
put out into the world

00:50:28.980 --> 00:50:30.600
and they really identify with it.

00:50:30.600 --> 00:50:33.060
To the point that they call
themselves Mythical Beasts

00:50:33.060 --> 00:50:35.340
and they will come to an event like this.

00:50:35.340 --> 00:50:36.750
But until you see it

00:50:36.750 --> 00:50:39.420
and you interact with these folks,

00:50:39.420 --> 00:50:40.890
you don't really appreciate it.

00:50:40.890 --> 00:50:45.420
I think it was dawning on
him repeatedly that this is,

00:50:45.420 --> 00:50:50.420
I didn't understand the
level of just commitment

00:50:52.973 --> 00:50:55.945
and the nature of the experience.

00:50:55.945 --> 00:50:57.233
That was cool to see.
And, of course, you know,

00:50:58.489 --> 00:51:01.860
we gave him a special role.

00:51:01.860 --> 00:51:02.693
Arguably the highlight.
Let's talk about,

00:51:02.693 --> 00:51:04.200
let's talk about the main show.

00:51:04.200 --> 00:51:05.703
So the main show,

00:51:07.860 --> 00:51:09.630
I'm glad we were able to stream it

00:51:09.630 --> 00:51:11.760
and then we were able
to do video on demand.

00:51:11.760 --> 00:51:13.110
You know, it's no longer available but,

00:51:13.110 --> 00:51:14.760
like, there were opportunities for people

00:51:14.760 --> 00:51:16.133
to experience it who weren't there.

00:51:16.133 --> 00:51:21.133
And I'm so glad because it
was the most fun I've ever had

00:51:24.000 --> 00:51:26.070
on a stage of ours, I think.

00:51:26.070 --> 00:51:28.890
I think it was our most
fun show we've ever done.

00:51:28.890 --> 00:51:29.723
Oh yeah.

00:51:29.723 --> 00:51:33.420
Like, from a personal
standpoint, my experience, like,

00:51:33.420 --> 00:51:34.800
everything that we've learned

00:51:34.800 --> 00:51:36.780
in our couple of years of touring

00:51:36.780 --> 00:51:39.720
and doing, like, three
different stage shows.

00:51:39.720 --> 00:51:42.513
We've done, like, very technical,

00:51:43.500 --> 00:51:46.530
like, polished presentation.

00:51:46.530 --> 00:51:48.030
And then with our last,

00:51:48.030 --> 00:51:49.320
and then we did a Bleak Creek thing,

00:51:49.320 --> 00:51:51.420
which was like more intimate
and totally different.

00:51:51.420 --> 00:51:52.830
But then like the Rhett &amp; Link tour,

00:51:52.830 --> 00:51:55.110
where it was just like music and banter

00:51:55.110 --> 00:51:57.450
is where we really started to learn

00:51:57.450 --> 00:52:00.930
that the looser, the better, you know.

00:52:00.930 --> 00:52:03.030
A lot of what we're
learning just with, like,

00:52:03.030 --> 00:52:05.730
Good Mythical Morning and
Good Mythical more in general,

00:52:05.730 --> 00:52:09.810
is that putting ourselves in environments

00:52:09.810 --> 00:52:12.540
where we can be ourselves
and interact with each other

00:52:12.540 --> 00:52:16.167
and interact with people is
where we do our best work.

00:52:16.167 --> 00:52:17.000
Right.

00:52:17.000 --> 00:52:18.090
And just surprise each other.

00:52:18.090 --> 00:52:20.850
And it's not about hitting
marks or certain lines.

00:52:20.850 --> 00:52:23.400
However, that all being said,

00:52:23.400 --> 00:52:26.670
there were from some very
important marks to be hit.

00:52:26.670 --> 00:52:27.503
Yeah.

00:52:27.503 --> 00:52:29.190
You know, because the show
consisted of, I think,

00:52:29.190 --> 00:52:32.940
five musical performances from
everything from, you know,

00:52:32.940 --> 00:52:34.770
starting out by coming
in on the double bike

00:52:34.770 --> 00:52:36.690
and doing the 'I'm on Vacation',

00:52:36.690 --> 00:52:38.970
to a few acoustic performances,

00:52:38.970 --> 00:52:42.840
to closing it out with a
black light, glow in the dark

00:52:42.840 --> 00:52:44.880
recreation of our 'So Dang Dark' video,

00:52:44.880 --> 00:52:47.430
that was super fun to put together.

00:52:47.430 --> 00:52:50.310
Intermingled or intermixed with

00:52:50.310 --> 00:52:51.510
something we've never done before,

00:52:51.510 --> 00:52:54.270
which is bringing a segment
of GMM live on stage

00:52:54.270 --> 00:52:55.740
in the international dart game.

00:52:55.740 --> 00:52:57.997
When we sang the

00:52:57.997 --> 00:53:01.290
"Where in the world do these
Mythical Beasts come from?",

00:53:01.290 --> 00:53:03.630
and then the doors open

00:53:03.630 --> 00:53:07.863
and Chase the cartographer
came out with that map, like,

00:53:08.820 --> 00:53:11.173
five times as big as the normal map.

00:53:11.173 --> 00:53:13.000
They went crazy for Chase.
Oh yeah.

00:53:13.000 --> 00:53:14.880
Like, I was so happy for Chase

00:53:14.880 --> 00:53:17.422
that, like, he had such
a moment, you know?

00:53:17.422 --> 00:53:18.255
Oh yeah.

00:53:18.255 --> 00:53:19.590
It was wonderful.

00:53:19.590 --> 00:53:20.757
And then of course...

00:53:20.757 --> 00:53:22.290
And Stevie was on the mic.

00:53:22.290 --> 00:53:25.260
Yeah, so we'll talk about the alien part,

00:53:25.260 --> 00:53:27.340
but my point was is that

00:53:28.740 --> 00:53:30.300
there were a lot of marks to hit

00:53:30.300 --> 00:53:34.140
and there were a lot of
technical things to do correctly.

00:53:34.140 --> 00:53:34.973
Yeah.

00:53:34.973 --> 00:53:38.070
That, you know, when we did
our tour Mythicality show

00:53:38.070 --> 00:53:39.090
a few years ago,

00:53:39.090 --> 00:53:40.500
there was a two week rehearsal

00:53:40.500 --> 00:53:42.900
where we like rented out a
rehearsal space in Burbank,

00:53:42.900 --> 00:53:45.840
and we, like, literally two weeks

00:53:45.840 --> 00:53:50.130
of going there on and off
over the course of two weeks,

00:53:50.130 --> 00:53:51.750
getting all the marks down,

00:53:51.750 --> 00:53:53.940
so you can take this show
and take it on the road.

00:53:53.940 --> 00:53:57.270
This was two hours of a
rehearsal on Thursday night

00:53:57.270 --> 00:53:58.260
to be ready for this show.

00:53:58.260 --> 00:53:59.700
Now, we knew what we were gonna do

00:53:59.700 --> 00:54:01.204
and we had scripted it out.

00:54:01.204 --> 00:54:03.690
The parts that were, you know, outlined,

00:54:03.690 --> 00:54:06.300
it's not scripted, but, you know,

00:54:06.300 --> 00:54:07.950
when your dad showed up at MythiCon,

00:54:07.950 --> 00:54:10.740
he didn't know he was gonna be an alien.

00:54:10.740 --> 00:54:12.180
No, I didn't tell him.

00:54:12.180 --> 00:54:14.640
I was like, I'll find the
right time to tell him.

00:54:14.640 --> 00:54:15.840
Which ended up being perfect

00:54:15.840 --> 00:54:16.976
for reasons we'll explain.
Which was at dinner

00:54:16.976 --> 00:54:19.230
in front of everybody.

00:54:19.230 --> 00:54:20.700
But what we knew we wanted to do was,

00:54:20.700 --> 00:54:23.400
of course, we had this idea.

00:54:23.400 --> 00:54:24.840
And he didn't bat an eye.

00:54:24.840 --> 00:54:28.530
Oh no. And I didn't think he would,

00:54:28.530 --> 00:54:30.150
but, like, now knowing
the way he interacts

00:54:30.150 --> 00:54:30.983
with these ideas,

00:54:30.983 --> 00:54:32.130
it's like we could get him to do anything.

00:54:32.130 --> 00:54:33.270
Oh yeah.

00:54:33.270 --> 00:54:34.500
But we knew...
Very dangerous.

00:54:34.500 --> 00:54:36.420
Wanted to play around with this idea

00:54:36.420 --> 00:54:39.210
that is an idea that
some people believe in.

00:54:39.210 --> 00:54:41.880
I don't personally or
necessarily believe in it,

00:54:41.880 --> 00:54:42.810
but this idea that you can

00:54:42.810 --> 00:54:44.850
connect with aliens through meditation.

00:54:44.850 --> 00:54:49.850
We were like, oh, that's a fun
sort of foil to do this set

00:54:49.950 --> 00:54:51.900
where we can kind of do some improv music

00:54:51.900 --> 00:54:54.330
where we're, like,
helping the alien zero in.

00:54:54.330 --> 00:54:56.010
Now, I'll just go ahead and say,

00:54:56.010 --> 00:54:58.459
if you were watching on the live stream

00:54:58.459 --> 00:55:02.070
and you felt like that
segment just went on too long,

00:55:02.070 --> 00:55:05.523
well, it wasn't primarily for you.

00:55:06.720 --> 00:55:08.760
The show was primarily
Couldn't be at that moment.

00:55:08.760 --> 00:55:11.010
for the people who were there, right?

00:55:11.010 --> 00:55:12.660
Who were participating in that chant,

00:55:12.660 --> 00:55:14.280
who were looking to the skies.

00:55:14.280 --> 00:55:16.170
And we said, listen, this
is primarily for the people

00:55:16.170 --> 00:55:17.520
who showed up to MythiCon,

00:55:17.520 --> 00:55:19.590
but we're gonna offer it as a live stream.

00:55:19.590 --> 00:55:22.200
Because if you wanna feel like
you're part of it, you can.

00:55:22.200 --> 00:55:23.430
But I can imagine...

00:55:23.430 --> 00:55:25.740
Especially that moment of, like, you know,

00:55:25.740 --> 00:55:27.270
group meditation, that's not something

00:55:27.270 --> 00:55:29.550
that people like stream on the internet

00:55:29.550 --> 00:55:30.930
for like thousands of views.

00:55:30.930 --> 00:55:32.070
And I'm sure people who were there

00:55:32.070 --> 00:55:33.360
thought it was going on for a long time.

00:55:33.360 --> 00:55:34.193
But again...

00:55:34.193 --> 00:55:35.026
What's your hobby?

00:55:35.026 --> 00:55:36.510
I like to watch other
people meditate live.

00:55:36.510 --> 00:55:39.300
We were doing, again,
none of that was written.

00:55:39.300 --> 00:55:40.560
The only thing that was pre-written

00:55:40.560 --> 00:55:42.450
were the coordinates that Jenna had found

00:55:42.450 --> 00:55:44.940
of the exact spot that
we were at, at Star Hill.

00:55:44.940 --> 00:55:48.270
And they were on my shruti
box taped on my shruti box.

00:55:48.270 --> 00:55:50.070
That's why I was reading my shruti box.

00:55:50.070 --> 00:55:51.000
I was playing it.

00:55:51.000 --> 00:55:53.790
Which is why, when that person,

00:55:53.790 --> 00:55:56.190
and we both retweeted the thing,

00:55:56.190 --> 00:55:59.820
like, leading up to MythiCon,

00:55:59.820 --> 00:56:01.770
somebody tweeted that video

00:56:01.770 --> 00:56:03.570
of this, like, hippie looking dude

00:56:03.570 --> 00:56:04.440
playing this instrument

00:56:04.440 --> 00:56:07.200
and then another dude
blowing bubbles on stage.

00:56:07.200 --> 00:56:10.080
And I was like, this is
basically our version,

00:56:10.080 --> 00:56:10.913
we've already planned our version of this.

00:56:10.913 --> 00:56:11.880
We were already ready for it.

00:56:11.880 --> 00:56:14.137
And so like, I love the
fact that somebody said

00:56:14.137 --> 00:56:16.560
"This is gonna be Rhett and
Link in X number of years".

00:56:16.560 --> 00:56:17.760
And I'm like, "No, this
is gonna be Rhett and Link

00:56:17.760 --> 00:56:18.593
in X number of weeks".

00:56:18.593 --> 00:56:19.740
Yeah, yeah, just get ready for it.

00:56:19.740 --> 00:56:21.000
So it was so weird.

00:56:21.000 --> 00:56:23.070
And we were like, yeah, set it up.

00:56:23.070 --> 00:56:28.050
Like, listen, you're here,
you're up for anything.

00:56:28.050 --> 00:56:30.120
So we just wanna take
full advantage of that.

00:56:30.120 --> 00:56:32.340
So now we are going to contact aliens.

00:56:32.340 --> 00:56:34.950
And then everybody was up for it.

00:56:34.950 --> 00:56:36.240
Oh, they were so up for it.

00:56:36.240 --> 00:56:37.350
Now...

00:56:37.350 --> 00:56:39.000
It was so ridiculous.
When we originally

00:56:39.000 --> 00:56:41.580
conceptualized this, it was just, hey,

00:56:41.580 --> 00:56:43.209
we're gonna do this thing of,

00:56:43.209 --> 00:56:44.880
we're trying to contact aliens.

00:56:44.880 --> 00:56:46.440
Obviously, it's gonna fail,

00:56:46.440 --> 00:56:49.953
but then it's gonna result in
Charles, an alien, showing up.

00:56:50.940 --> 00:56:53.340
Now we wanted it to be something

00:56:53.340 --> 00:56:55.230
where we might be able to fool some people

00:56:55.230 --> 00:56:57.600
or give you the emotional feeling

00:56:57.600 --> 00:56:59.250
of that you have made contact.

00:56:59.250 --> 00:57:00.660
And so the first thing we looked into

00:57:00.660 --> 00:57:02.463
was like a drone show.

00:57:03.300 --> 00:57:04.380
Those are...

00:57:04.380 --> 00:57:07.083
It's when you have, like,
if you don't know, like,

00:57:08.460 --> 00:57:13.440
could be 20 or 30 or hundreds
of drones that are programmed.

00:57:13.440 --> 00:57:15.090
To like make a giant face in the sky.

00:57:15.090 --> 00:57:15.923
They did it,

00:57:15.923 --> 00:57:18.709
if you look up 'drone show, Burning Man'.

00:57:18.709 --> 00:57:19.542
It's awesome.

00:57:19.542 --> 00:57:20.580
There's a giant face.

00:57:20.580 --> 00:57:24.120
I mean, it's like the face
of God looking down at you.

00:57:24.120 --> 00:57:24.953
So crazy.

00:57:24.953 --> 00:57:26.490
Now, we already indicated earlier

00:57:26.490 --> 00:57:28.803
that we did not make any money.

00:57:29.910 --> 00:57:32.040
So we weren't about to spend the, like,

00:57:32.040 --> 00:57:35.167
approximately $1 million
that it would've cost

00:57:35.167 --> 00:57:37.530
in order to, if you get
one of these companies

00:57:37.530 --> 00:57:40.560
to do the type of drone
show that they do for, like,

00:57:40.560 --> 00:57:42.030
the NBA finals or something.

00:57:42.030 --> 00:57:45.870
So we quickly downgraded
to one singular drone

00:57:45.870 --> 00:57:46.743
that we rented.

00:57:48.000 --> 00:57:49.470
And you may think, "Oh, okay".

00:57:49.470 --> 00:57:50.613
But if you were there.

00:57:51.617 --> 00:57:53.280
And if you were really close,

00:57:53.280 --> 00:57:54.420
if you were like on the front row,

00:57:54.420 --> 00:57:56.610
the stage is actually too
big for you to see it,

00:57:56.610 --> 00:57:58.110
so you needed to be back,
kind of in the middle,

00:57:58.110 --> 00:57:59.280
then everybody in the back.

00:57:59.280 --> 00:58:02.889
I went back there during
the rehearsal and watched it

00:58:02.889 --> 00:58:04.560
and I was like, "Oh gosh,
this is gonna be great".

00:58:04.560 --> 00:58:07.350
Yeah, Ben rented a drone,
he piloted the drone.

00:58:07.350 --> 00:58:08.550
And it had a big spotlight on it.

00:58:08.550 --> 00:58:09.383
So it was very bright.

00:58:09.383 --> 00:58:13.410
It was like a search and
rescue drone spotlight.

00:58:13.410 --> 00:58:14.243
Yeah.

00:58:14.243 --> 00:58:16.860
Well, and apparently, it
was so convincing that

00:58:16.860 --> 00:58:18.480
when we were doing our brunch

00:58:18.480 --> 00:58:21.600
the next morning, on Sunday morning,

00:58:21.600 --> 00:58:23.580
we were saying like, "Hey,
what was your highlight?".

00:58:23.580 --> 00:58:26.400
A lot of people said the show last night,

00:58:26.400 --> 00:58:28.181
just absolutely loved it.

00:58:28.181 --> 00:58:30.447
And then one person piped up and said,

00:58:30.447 --> 00:58:33.000
"Do you guys know that right

00:58:33.000 --> 00:58:34.470
when you were doing that thing

00:58:34.470 --> 00:58:36.570
where you contacted aliens,

00:58:36.570 --> 00:58:40.050
a plane flew over really, really low".

00:58:40.050 --> 00:58:41.100
It freaked me out.

00:58:41.100 --> 00:58:43.590
I thought maybe something
we had done had worked.

00:58:43.590 --> 00:58:44.423
We were like,

00:58:44.423 --> 00:58:45.256
Really?

00:58:46.381 --> 00:58:48.750
You sure it wasn't a UFO

00:58:48.750 --> 00:58:50.430
that then landed behind the stage?

00:58:50.430 --> 00:58:52.440
Right, so the way it happened was

00:58:52.440 --> 00:58:55.560
is that we're singing the coordinates,

00:58:55.560 --> 00:58:57.157
chanting the coordinates, then chanting,

00:58:57.157 --> 00:58:58.920
"We are looking to the skies for you".

00:58:58.920 --> 00:59:02.160
At which point Ben kind of
brings the drone over the stage

00:59:02.160 --> 00:59:04.590
and then slowly lowers
it down behind the stage.

00:59:04.590 --> 00:59:06.990
And this is all choreographed
with this sound design

00:59:06.990 --> 00:59:08.100
that Ben has put together,

00:59:08.100 --> 00:59:10.440
including this really low, if you, again,

00:59:10.440 --> 00:59:12.030
this is one of those things that, like,

00:59:12.030 --> 00:59:14.340
why being there was so much different,

00:59:14.340 --> 00:59:17.340
because they started
playing this very low rumble

00:59:17.340 --> 00:59:19.533
that was shaking the entire place

00:59:19.533 --> 00:59:22.983
as this drone is coming down, right?

00:59:24.030 --> 00:59:24.863
Yeah.

00:59:24.863 --> 00:59:26.910
Then when it lands, there's this,

00:59:26.910 --> 00:59:31.290
it feels like this light comes
on from behind the stage.

00:59:31.290 --> 00:59:32.670
And then, you know, it took a while

00:59:32.670 --> 00:59:33.660
to kind of get things situated.

00:59:33.660 --> 00:59:35.220
We were kind of waiting there for a while,

00:59:35.220 --> 00:59:38.923
but eventually the doors open,
CO2 cannons, boom, light,

00:59:38.923 --> 00:59:42.450
and then an alien with
a giant fricking head,

00:59:42.450 --> 00:59:45.660
like an eight foot tall
alien with four arms

00:59:45.660 --> 00:59:48.414
is standing there with
a three ring binder.

00:59:50.387 --> 00:59:52.950
Yeah the alien comes out

00:59:52.950 --> 00:59:55.800
and just kind of, like, shuffles forward.

00:59:55.800 --> 01:00:00.750
He lifts his forearms with
the tablet in his left hand

01:00:00.750 --> 01:00:01.583
and he says...

01:00:01.583 --> 01:00:02.416
Binder.

01:00:02.416 --> 01:00:03.249
A binder.

01:00:04.842 --> 01:00:06.810
"Greetings earthlings".

01:00:06.810 --> 01:00:07.739
I come in peace.

01:00:07.739 --> 01:00:09.270
"I come in peace".

01:00:09.270 --> 01:00:11.325
I don't think dad
changed his voice at all.

01:00:11.325 --> 01:00:14.400
I'd say like a 10% change.

01:00:14.400 --> 01:00:16.140
But this is where...

01:00:16.140 --> 01:00:16.973
So great.

01:00:16.973 --> 01:00:21.758
...your dad being your dad
really made this segment.

01:00:23.670 --> 01:00:27.330
And I think the best way to illustrate

01:00:27.330 --> 01:00:29.700
what it's like to work with
your dad in this context

01:00:29.700 --> 01:00:31.250
is to talk about the rehearsal.

01:00:32.760 --> 01:00:34.002
Which, basically, we had...

01:00:34.002 --> 01:00:35.662
Welcome to your future.

01:00:35.662 --> 01:00:38.040
We had a little discussion with him

01:00:38.040 --> 01:00:39.517
and it was essentially,

01:00:39.517 --> 01:00:41.850
"Charles, you know, the
doors are gonna open,

01:00:41.850 --> 01:00:43.050
you're gonna come out, you're gonna say,

01:00:43.050 --> 01:00:44.733
greetings, I come in peace.

01:00:46.080 --> 01:00:47.640
Then we're gonna have a little discussion

01:00:47.640 --> 01:00:49.140
with you about where do you come from,

01:00:49.140 --> 01:00:50.880
what's your name?

01:00:50.880 --> 01:00:54.600
And then you're going to say
that I brought you a gift,

01:00:54.600 --> 01:00:56.070
it's the time gate.

01:00:56.070 --> 01:00:58.350
And then this big sort
of elevator looking thing

01:00:58.350 --> 01:01:00.420
is gonna kind of come out from the door.

01:01:00.420 --> 01:01:02.940
And then you're gonna,
there's an instruction manual

01:01:02.940 --> 01:01:03.900
that you're gonna read.

01:01:03.900 --> 01:01:05.610
It's a three ring binder,

01:01:05.610 --> 01:01:07.920
that explains basically why
you're giving us to this

01:01:07.920 --> 01:01:08.753
and what we can do.

01:01:08.753 --> 01:01:09.586
And it sets up the next bit".

01:01:09.586 --> 01:01:11.910
"You don't have to memorize
how the time gate works,

01:01:11.910 --> 01:01:14.250
you just read it off the manual".

01:01:14.250 --> 01:01:15.570
And the first

01:01:15.570 --> 01:01:18.363
So, during the rehearsal he walks out,

01:01:19.290 --> 01:01:21.757
he spreads his arms and he says,

01:01:21.757 --> 01:01:23.187
"Welcome earthlings".

01:01:26.430 --> 01:01:28.267
As if we're all the ones that showed up.

01:01:28.267 --> 01:01:31.266
"Welcome to this planet I just arrived at"

01:01:31.266 --> 01:01:32.430
"That you're already on".

01:01:32.430 --> 01:01:33.263
I was like, "Oh".

01:01:33.263 --> 01:01:35.074
Okay, a little twist there.
I like that choice.

01:01:35.074 --> 01:01:36.090
A little twist.

01:01:36.090 --> 01:01:37.020
And we told him that

01:01:37.020 --> 01:01:38.280
when we ask him what his name is,

01:01:38.280 --> 01:01:41.790
that his name is Darrell in our language.

01:01:41.790 --> 01:01:42.840
Well we said...

01:01:42.840 --> 01:01:43.673
So then we asked him.

01:01:43.673 --> 01:01:44.550
It's too difficult for you

01:01:44.550 --> 01:01:45.960
to understand it in my language,

01:01:45.960 --> 01:01:47.760
but it's the equivalent of Darrell.

01:01:47.760 --> 01:01:48.882
Is what we told him.

01:01:48.882 --> 01:01:49.715
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:01:49.715 --> 01:01:50.820
And so then when we're
doing the rehearsal,

01:01:50.820 --> 01:01:51.870
I'm like, "Well, what's your name?"

01:01:51.870 --> 01:01:54.617
And he was like, "Charles".

01:01:55.710 --> 01:01:57.300
Which I was like, that's perfect.

01:01:57.300 --> 01:01:58.980
Like, didn't wanna change any of that.

01:01:58.980 --> 01:02:02.310
So, he got it all right during
the show, unfortunately.

01:02:02.310 --> 01:02:06.150
Because you kept telling me
to, like, to help him out,

01:02:06.150 --> 01:02:08.280
to, like, make sure he had
the right lines, which he did.

01:02:08.280 --> 01:02:09.113
Well that wasn't...

01:02:09.113 --> 01:02:10.023
I think it would've been
even funnier if he didn't.

01:02:10.023 --> 01:02:12.360
That actually wasn't...

01:02:12.360 --> 01:02:14.760
He wasn't supposed to say
that his name was Darrell.

01:02:14.760 --> 01:02:16.957
He was supposed to say that,

01:02:16.957 --> 01:02:19.080
"I can't pronounce my
name in your language",

01:02:19.080 --> 01:02:20.100
but he just said Darrell,

01:02:20.100 --> 01:02:21.840
which ended up getting a laugh,

01:02:21.840 --> 01:02:24.150
but the funniest part of the rehearsal

01:02:24.150 --> 01:02:26.280
was when we said, "Did you bring a gift?".

01:02:26.280 --> 01:02:27.667
And he holds up the binder and he's like,

01:02:27.667 --> 01:02:29.427
"I brought this book".

01:02:31.380 --> 01:02:34.230
And it was, the gift is the book.

01:02:34.230 --> 01:02:35.613
We're like, "No, no, no, no, no, no.

01:02:35.613 --> 01:02:37.170
The gift is the time gate".

01:02:37.170 --> 01:02:38.233
And that's the instruction manual.

01:02:38.233 --> 01:02:40.710
The book's the instruction manual.

01:02:40.710 --> 01:02:45.450
And it seemed like it was
difficult for him to get.

01:02:45.450 --> 01:02:47.370
To restore that information in his brain.

01:02:47.370 --> 01:02:51.750
But he did, he did by the time
we got around to the show.

01:02:51.750 --> 01:02:53.280
I'm just glad he didn't trip and fall.

01:02:53.280 --> 01:02:55.260
You know, like bust his brain open

01:02:55.260 --> 01:02:56.970
and I'm talking about
that huge alien brain

01:02:56.970 --> 01:02:58.470
that was, like, his whole face

01:02:58.470 --> 01:03:00.390
was in the mouth of the alien.

01:03:00.390 --> 01:03:01.740
So funny.

01:03:01.740 --> 01:03:03.150
I was so proud of him, he did awesome.

01:03:03.150 --> 01:03:04.440
He loved every second of it.

01:03:04.440 --> 01:03:05.413
Yeah.

01:03:05.413 --> 01:03:06.246
It was great.

01:03:06.246 --> 01:03:08.220
And then Jordan...

01:03:08.220 --> 01:03:09.053
Was in the time gate.

01:03:09.053 --> 01:03:09.886
Of course, the bit was

01:03:09.886 --> 01:03:11.640
you can communicate with your past selves,

01:03:11.640 --> 01:03:13.320
your present selves or your future selves.

01:03:13.320 --> 01:03:15.390
And so we kind of brought in

01:03:15.390 --> 01:03:18.000
what Jordan has really done
a good job with on GMM,

01:03:18.000 --> 01:03:20.850
which is putting his mouth up to a hole.

01:03:20.850 --> 01:03:22.587
And saying something hilarious.

01:03:22.587 --> 01:03:25.560
And so he played young me and young you

01:03:25.560 --> 01:03:26.860
and then old me and old you.

01:03:26.860 --> 01:03:29.850
Yeah, but we each played present us.

01:03:29.850 --> 01:03:31.880
And then whatever the other
one of us was as well.

01:03:31.880 --> 01:03:32.713
It was fun.

01:03:32.713 --> 01:03:36.300
It was a good ending for, I mean,

01:03:36.300 --> 01:03:37.830
that was fun, that was a nice bit.

01:03:37.830 --> 01:03:39.090
And then we, like, sang

01:03:39.090 --> 01:03:40.890
'Lonely Sad Little Water Dispensing Tab',

01:03:40.890 --> 01:03:42.510
did a little singalong.

01:03:42.510 --> 01:03:44.190
And then we went away, we put on our suits

01:03:44.190 --> 01:03:45.930
and we did the encore.

01:03:45.930 --> 01:03:47.130
Did 'So Dang Dark'.

01:03:47.130 --> 01:03:48.720
I almost stepped off of the stage.

01:03:48.720 --> 01:03:50.850
I will say that, because during the encore

01:03:50.850 --> 01:03:52.800
we had to go back and change.

01:03:52.800 --> 01:03:53.633
And again these are...

01:03:53.633 --> 01:03:54.466
That would've been bad.

01:03:54.466 --> 01:03:55.500
There are these little things

01:03:55.500 --> 01:03:59.460
that you work out doing a rehearsal,

01:03:59.460 --> 01:04:00.660
even a second rehearsal,

01:04:00.660 --> 01:04:02.310
that we just didn't, we didn't have time,

01:04:02.310 --> 01:04:04.170
because we got there and here's the stage

01:04:04.170 --> 01:04:06.030
and all the stuff's coming together,

01:04:06.030 --> 01:04:08.790
this stuff has just
been built just in time.

01:04:08.790 --> 01:04:10.920
So when we go back to
change out of our clothes

01:04:10.920 --> 01:04:13.290
into these skin tight clothes

01:04:13.290 --> 01:04:16.440
that have black light
reflective paint on them

01:04:16.440 --> 01:04:18.150
that mimics exactly the way we looked

01:04:18.150 --> 01:04:19.833
in the 'So Dang Dark' video.

01:04:21.600 --> 01:04:23.610
There were no lights.

01:04:23.610 --> 01:04:25.950
And so there were steps down to this area

01:04:25.950 --> 01:04:27.300
that we were gonna change

01:04:27.300 --> 01:04:29.280
and I kind of knew that
there were steps there

01:04:29.280 --> 01:04:31.470
and I didn't know that there were, like,

01:04:31.470 --> 01:04:33.630
they were kind of not that wide.

01:04:33.630 --> 01:04:34.860
And so when I stepped down,

01:04:34.860 --> 01:04:37.860
I stepped completely
down to the next level.

01:04:37.860 --> 01:04:40.920
And, I mean, that could
have been catastrophic,

01:04:40.920 --> 01:04:41.853
but it just happened.

01:04:41.853 --> 01:04:42.686
You could have broken your hip.

01:04:42.686 --> 01:04:45.210
I caught myself and
that was the only moment

01:04:45.210 --> 01:04:46.043
that I felt unsure about this.

01:04:46.043 --> 01:04:47.550
Again, nobody left on a stretcher.

01:04:47.550 --> 01:04:49.260
Success.
Not even us.

01:04:49.260 --> 01:04:50.820
Success.

01:04:50.820 --> 01:04:54.720
And, you know, after that was my DJ set

01:04:54.720 --> 01:04:55.553
to the close the thing out,

01:04:55.553 --> 01:04:58.143
which we'll talk about next episode.

01:04:59.250 --> 01:05:00.120
And then the next morning,

01:05:00.120 --> 01:05:01.440
yeah, we had a brunch with, like,

01:05:01.440 --> 01:05:03.030
people who had that ticket

01:05:03.030 --> 01:05:04.080
and like you said,

01:05:04.080 --> 01:05:05.149
we went around and talked to

01:05:05.149 --> 01:05:08.280
a bunch of Mythical Beasts
as they were eating,

01:05:08.280 --> 01:05:09.330
wedding reception style.

01:05:09.330 --> 01:05:12.330
And also, the Mythical
crew was there for that,

01:05:12.330 --> 01:05:14.250
our wives were there for that.

01:05:14.250 --> 01:05:18.240
Really the first time
that Jessie and Christie

01:05:18.240 --> 01:05:20.720
have interacted with Mythical Beasts

01:05:20.720 --> 01:05:21.960
in that way, I think.

01:05:21.960 --> 01:05:23.250
Yeah, yeah.

01:05:23.250 --> 01:05:26.280
And, you know, the fact that, you know,

01:05:26.280 --> 01:05:28.620
Lily and Lincoln and Lando were also there

01:05:28.620 --> 01:05:29.700
was very special to me.

01:05:29.700 --> 01:05:30.997
I know Locke couldn't
make it because he was,

01:05:30.997 --> 01:05:32.880
you know, just getting going with school.

01:05:32.880 --> 01:05:33.930
But Shepherd was there.

01:05:33.930 --> 01:05:35.130
And I'm just so glad that they were there

01:05:35.130 --> 01:05:36.570
it was a big part of it.

01:05:36.570 --> 01:05:38.700
Like, Lily posted a picture of, like,

01:05:38.700 --> 01:05:40.140
me getting ready for my DJ set,

01:05:40.140 --> 01:05:41.910
when we're like hanging
out on this bed, like,

01:05:41.910 --> 01:05:44.610
in one of the places the day before.

01:05:44.610 --> 01:05:45.720
It's just like, you know,

01:05:45.720 --> 01:05:49.170
these are all these, like,
great memories for me

01:05:49.170 --> 01:05:52.080
that the moment I took that initial tour

01:05:52.080 --> 01:05:54.210
and walked around right
when we arrived, I was like,

01:05:54.210 --> 01:05:57.270
I'm so glad that my family's
showing up for this thing

01:05:57.270 --> 01:06:00.630
and experiencing this because you know,

01:06:00.630 --> 01:06:03.870
whatever we do next will be different.

01:06:03.870 --> 01:06:06.210
You know, that's how we are.

01:06:06.210 --> 01:06:07.980
So it'll be enough the same,

01:06:07.980 --> 01:06:10.710
but enough different to still be exciting.

01:06:10.710 --> 01:06:13.050
But to be the first one,
for it to be so special,

01:06:13.050 --> 01:06:15.570
you know, special for
everybody who was there

01:06:15.570 --> 01:06:17.970
and to have, like, our family there

01:06:17.970 --> 01:06:20.133
to have so many of the
Mythical crew there.

01:06:21.096 --> 01:06:23.430
And that they had good experiences.

01:06:23.430 --> 01:06:27.720
It was, it all just, you
know, it was hot stress

01:06:27.720 --> 01:06:32.280
and people had to work
themselves to the bone.

01:06:32.280 --> 01:06:34.143
I acknowledge that, but like,

01:06:36.570 --> 01:06:38.850
in my opinion it was
all worth it, you know,

01:06:38.850 --> 01:06:40.620
from my perspective, I'm just so proud

01:06:40.620 --> 01:06:41.880
of what everybody did to put into it

01:06:41.880 --> 01:06:46.530
and that we got to experience it together.

01:06:46.530 --> 01:06:48.748
It was just, yeah, it was awesome.

01:06:48.748 --> 01:06:53.748
It was absolutely a
career highlight for me.

01:06:53.820 --> 01:06:54.653
Oh yeah.

01:06:54.653 --> 01:06:56.400
Yes, thank you to everybody
who was able to make it.

01:06:56.400 --> 01:06:58.320
We know that, you know,
in the scheme of things,

01:06:58.320 --> 01:07:01.867
it was a relatively small
percentage of Mythical Beasts.

01:07:01.867 --> 01:07:03.840
But you know, many of you,

01:07:03.840 --> 01:07:08.100
who are some of our most
active Mythical Beasts who,

01:07:08.100 --> 01:07:12.420
you know, make their passion
for all things Mythical

01:07:12.420 --> 01:07:14.130
known on the internet on a regular basis,

01:07:14.130 --> 01:07:15.210
a lot of you were there.

01:07:15.210 --> 01:07:16.470
People that we've met before,

01:07:16.470 --> 01:07:19.200
previous tours and meet and greets.

01:07:19.200 --> 01:07:21.960
And a lot of people we haven't met before.

01:07:21.960 --> 01:07:24.900
Obviously, we couldn't do this without you

01:07:24.900 --> 01:07:28.080
and you made it a very, very special thing

01:07:28.080 --> 01:07:30.597
that we're gonna take
everything that we learned

01:07:30.597 --> 01:07:33.750
and all the things that
made it really special,

01:07:33.750 --> 01:07:37.620
made it unique, all the best parts of it

01:07:37.620 --> 01:07:41.400
and try to find ways to
replicate that moving forward.

01:07:41.400 --> 01:07:42.270
What that's gonna look like,

01:07:42.270 --> 01:07:44.850
like you said, we don't know.

01:07:44.850 --> 01:07:48.660
But we always love to start
something, learn from it,

01:07:48.660 --> 01:07:52.600
keep moving forward and
there's nothing like

01:07:54.660 --> 01:07:57.287
when we all get together like that.

01:07:57.287 --> 01:07:58.937
And we say it every time we tour.

01:08:00.300 --> 01:08:01.133
Yeah.

01:08:01.133 --> 01:08:01.966
But it's just, it remains true

01:08:01.966 --> 01:08:04.260
that there's nothing like
being in the same place

01:08:04.260 --> 01:08:05.860
with a bunch of Mythical Beasts.

01:08:07.020 --> 01:08:10.380
Especially after everything
that, like, you know,

01:08:10.380 --> 01:08:11.976
with the pandemic of it all, you know.

01:08:11.976 --> 01:08:12.809
Yeah.

01:08:12.809 --> 01:08:16.860
It's really, it gave us
a renewed appreciation

01:08:16.860 --> 01:08:21.570
for being there and having the
ability to create something,

01:08:21.570 --> 01:08:24.660
have people show up and collaboratively

01:08:24.660 --> 01:08:26.910
make it an experience.

01:08:26.910 --> 01:08:27.743
Yeah.

01:08:27.743 --> 01:08:29.403
You know, so very grateful.

01:08:30.270 --> 01:08:32.430
I feel like we took our own advice,

01:08:32.430 --> 01:08:35.070
we stopped, we celebrated.

01:08:35.070 --> 01:08:37.080
We're gonna do more of that
in the next episode too.

